1
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:04,720
October was absolutely insane for AI.

2
00:00:04,974 --> 00:00:17,060
OpenAI revealed a mysterious system behind 722 mathematical papers, while Anthropics Claude started designing quantum circuits better than human experts, and Google made huge breakthroughs with its Willow chip.

3
00:00:17,518 --> 00:00:18,788
And Google wasn't done.

4
00:00:19,054 --> 00:00:29,924
Its new Gemini 4 Argon spent 11 hours rewriting code on its own, made quantum computing dramatically more efficient, and even taught a robot new skills just by watching a video.

5
00:00:30,414 --> 00:00:39,204
Meanwhile, secret Claude models started appearing everywhere, from the mysterious Claude Next and Fable 5.5 to growing rumors about Opus 6.

6
00:00:39,918 --> 00:00:44,996
Then Anthropic's own co-founder declared that the AI singularity has already begun.

7
00:00:45,614 --> 00:00:54,628
Some AI insiders started discussing survival hideouts, while Sam Altman sparked outrage by suggesting society might have to accept some pretty bad outcomes from AI.

8
00:00:55,150 --> 00:01:04,452
And as if that wasn't enough, a massive cybersecurity crisis threatened millions of servers, with AI-powered attacks raising fears of an internet-wide nightmare.

9
00:01:04,942 --> 00:01:07,520
So yeah, October was completely wild.

10
00:01:07,726 --> 00:01:13,252
These are the biggest AI stories of the month, and when you put them all together, things look even crazier.

11
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:14,660
So let's talk about it.

12
00:01:16,174 --> 00:01:21,476
27 major cyber attacks and security incidents hit the internet in just 48 hours.

13
00:01:21,902 --> 00:01:25,156
Governments across Europe yanked their own websites offline.

14
00:01:25,678 --> 00:01:35,940
Japan's prime minister went on X asking regular people to check their servers, and a company that vouches for websites being safe turned out to be guarding its master keys with the number four.

15
00:01:36,334 --> 00:01:45,604
People are calling it the darkest October, and AI is in the middle of almost all of it, finding the holes, exploiting them, and sometimes wrecking things all by itself.

16
00:01:46,062 --> 00:01:51,652
So every app sits on a stack of software layers, and this week nearly every layer cracked.

17
00:01:52,238 --> 00:01:57,316
At the bottom is Linux, running most of the world's servers and basically the whole cloud.

18
00:01:57,742 --> 00:02:03,812
Cloud companies split one big machine into sealed-off virtual machines rented to different customers.

19
00:02:04,462 --> 00:02:13,444
On October 15th, a bug went public that let someone inside one of those break out and grab root, meaning full admin control over the host and everyone on it.

20
00:02:13,966 --> 00:02:21,520
An autonomous AI security engine from Pwn.ai found it, and Linus Torvalds merged the fix a day earlier.

21
00:02:21,774 --> 00:02:27,920
At another Linux bug handing regular users admin rights, plus a TIDB database flaw.

22
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:33,028
One layer up is Nginx, the software standing at the front door of millions of websites.

23
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:38,372
On October 14th, it patched five bugs, three rated 9.2 out of 10.

24
00:02:38,958 --> 00:02:48,640
Some let a crafted request overflow memory and run the attacker's code or crash the site, and one breaks the check where both sides show ID badges, so attackers walk right past it.

25
00:02:48,814 --> 00:02:55,012
Then GitLab, where tons of companies keep their code and the automated pipelines that push updates live.

26
00:02:55,438 --> 00:03:03,920
It got two perfect 10 out of 10 bugs on October 12th and 21st, letting anyone take over a self-hosted GitLab server without logging in.

27
00:03:04,078 --> 00:03:09,220
On October 20th, Chrome 156 shipped with 353 security fixes.

28
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:14,980
A year ago, Chrome 141 and 142 had 21 and 26.

29
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:21,604
Six were critical, including a 9.6 where a booby-trapped web page could escape Chrome's sandbox.

30
00:03:22,286 --> 00:03:32,260
Four of those six came from anthropic researcher Xin Young Jay, one found with Claude's help, and one more from OpenAI's Codex Security, so five of six came from AI companies.

31
00:03:32,558 --> 00:03:34,692
Then came the night of October 21st.

32
00:03:35,246 --> 00:03:38,340
Finland shut down all its government's online services.

33
00:03:38,926 --> 00:03:54,560
The Netherlands took down its digital ID system, Digid, government portals, the Port of Rotterdam, and several hospitals, and even the Dutch Cybersecurity Center switched off its own site, which is a bit like the fire department evacuating the fire station, but honestly, correct call.

34
00:03:55,054 --> 00:04:02,116
France ordered every provincial government site offline, and Germany, Belgium, Wikimedia, and Nintendo pulled services too.

35
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:12,516
In Japan, Pixiv and Nikonico went into emergency maintenance, the Sushi Chain Sushiro paused its ordering app, and Final Fantasy XIV players in China had to switch launchers.

36
00:04:13,038 --> 00:04:15,904
All of them unplugged themselves before anyone broke in.

37
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:19,904
The reason is PHP, the language running the back kitchen of the internet.

38
00:04:20,384 --> 00:04:25,540
Login, search, or comment on a site, and PHP is usually what's handling it.

39
00:04:25,904 --> 00:04:42,384
WordPress, Drupal, Joomla, Magento, and the software behind Wikipedia all run on it, and W3Tech says it powers 69.3% of websites with an identifiable backend, over 33 million sites, while Second Place sits at 8.1%.

40
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:52,836
The two bugs, nicknamed PHP2 Shell, let one request get an attacker a shell, a command line on your server, so total control, no password needed.

41
00:04:53,294 --> 00:04:57,252
Both are rated 9.2, and Watchtower gave them a full 10.

42
00:04:57,454 --> 00:04:59,684
The first one is about running out of memory.

43
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:11,940
PHP slams an emergency stop, and at exactly the wrong moment that leaves data half set up, which the cleanup treats as real, so on Apache, an attacker can run code before the website's own code even starts.

44
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:22,372
The second is about leftovers, where the next request reuses memory PHP already threw away, which can run code or leak other users' passwords and API keys.

45
00:05:22,958 --> 00:05:25,744
Wiz said, even a bare Hello World site is affected.

46
00:05:25,998 --> 00:05:35,012
Cloudflare says this goes back over a decade to PHP 5.4, and Volker Dush of the PHP Foundation compared it to Heartbleed in 2014.

47
00:05:35,534 --> 00:05:46,436
Patches only cover versions 8.2 through 8.5, while 27% of PHP sites still run version 7 and 7.7% run version 5, which get no fix.

48
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:56,580
The release was planned for October 22nd, but on the 20th it leaked on Hacker News and Breach Forums, Watchtower confirmed it within hours, and PHP moved the patch up a day.

49
00:05:57,038 --> 00:06:12,004
That night, Caliph, one of the finders, accidentally posted a demo of its attack hitting real targets, and next morning ChainGuard, a distributor with early access, broke its non-disclosure agreement and shipped the fix early, which is basically a treasure map for attackers.

50
00:06:12,558 --> 00:06:15,744
Cloudflare saw attacks six hours before the official patch.

51
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:25,024
The NSA, Britain, and the EU said to take unpatched sites offline, and Prime Minister Sanai Takaichi told Japan to get ready for the 11 pm patch.

52
00:06:25,198 --> 00:06:32,144
OpenAI then counted PHP 2 Shell as an AI win for GPT-5.6 Seoul, its cheaper model.

53
00:06:32,494 --> 00:06:39,332
Caliph says researcher Ryan Austin found it first on August 5th using public models, and his edge was history.

54
00:06:39,918 --> 00:06:43,904
Stefan Esser found a similar PHP memory bug in 2004.

55
00:06:44,206 --> 00:06:56,224
A related 2020 bug report got ignored, and a 2024 audit funded by Germany's sovereign tech agency spotted this exact flaw, but nobody could prove it was exploitable, and every fix broke something, so it stalled.

56
00:06:56,462 --> 00:06:59,424
Writer Nick Rogers calls this security by exhaustion.

57
00:06:59,598 --> 00:07:07,344
Turning a bug like this into a working attack took weeks of tedious manual debugging, so nobody bothered, and tons of old internet code is protected that way.

58
00:07:07,566 --> 00:07:12,384
Then in June, OpenAI's Patch the planet, started funding audits of open source software.

59
00:07:12,782 --> 00:07:18,564
Caliph picked PHP, and around August 1st, an AI agent spotted the key flaw within days.

60
00:07:19,150 --> 00:07:23,984
Then the AI turned that bug's pattern into a reusable skill file, which found the second bug.

61
00:07:24,174 --> 00:07:29,412
OpenAI independently reported the same root cause on August 11th, the same day as Caliph.

62
00:07:29,806 --> 00:07:45,188
Wiz, owned by Google, found it separately on August 19th with Gemini 4 Argon and GPT 5.6 Sol had working takeovers on Drupal and WordPress within two days, and said one researcher did in a week what used to take top teams months.

63
00:07:45,614 --> 00:07:54,820
Oh, and on October 21st, Node.js, which runs behind PayPal, Netflix, and LinkedIn, fixed 10 security bugs.

64
00:07:55,246 --> 00:07:59,584
The worst letting a malformed compressed file leak secrets or run code.

65
00:08:00,302 --> 00:08:05,444
And Go patched a flaw, letting forged certificates trick cloud services into trusting each other.

66
00:08:05,616 --> 00:08:06,948
Now, the number four.

67
00:08:07,342 --> 00:08:14,404
The padlock in your browser means your data is encrypted, so the routers between you and a website pass it along without reading it.

68
00:08:14,830 --> 00:08:18,852
That relies on certificate authorities, trusted middlemen vouching for websites.

69
00:08:19,470 --> 00:08:30,020
eMudra, an Indian one, had eight intermediate certificates able to vouch for almost any site with private keys that should have two to the power of 384 possibilities.

70
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:35,456
Instead, every byte was zero except the last, leaving 256.

71
00:08:36,110 --> 00:08:45,028
Researcher Scott Helms said one key was just four, others were 56 and 115, and he cracked them all on his laptop in under a second.

72
00:08:45,454 --> 00:08:52,708
Others found 66, 155, 156, 230, and 237.

73
00:08:53,134 --> 00:08:58,016
The combination lock on my gym bag has a thousand codes, so my socks were better protected.

74
00:08:58,222 --> 00:09:06,084
With those keys, someone could make a fake bank site look legit, or sit on your network reading your chats and injecting fake you won a prize pop-ups.

75
00:09:06,766 --> 00:09:13,348
Chrome, Firefox, Apple, and Microsoft began distrusting the certificates from the 17th.

76
00:09:13,774 --> 00:09:17,252
Apple revoked them all on the 21st and restored three a day later.

77
00:09:17,614 --> 00:09:22,852
Firefox pulled 12, and six days in, WeChat's built-in browser still accepted one.

78
00:09:23,342 --> 00:09:26,176
You can check your browser at Emudra, we have a problem.

79
00:09:26,510 --> 00:09:33,924
Emudra's stock fell its 20% daily limit, and the cause was staff forgetting to swap out a weak key used for migration testing.

80
00:09:34,414 --> 00:09:40,548
Smart cameras, speakers, and old servers that never update will keep trusting these certificates for years.

81
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:42,756
AI keys are the new target too.

82
00:09:43,214 --> 00:10:01,636
Light LLM, which connects apps to AI models and gets 23 million downloads a week, had version 1.107.0 poisoned through stolen publishing credentials, so the GitHub code looked clean while what you installed was infected, and it usually holds your API keys.

83
00:10:02,254 --> 00:10:17,732
On October 17th, the coding tool OpenCode was breached, possibly exposing OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google keys for 1.4% of its console users in what it tied to a distillation attack, basically using stolen access to copy a model.

84
00:10:18,254 --> 00:10:22,336
Some users woke up to bills from a few hundred to over $1,700.

85
00:10:23,182 --> 00:10:37,668
On October 21st, the hosting platform Railway said an outsider decrypted some customers' stored passwords and API keys, likely to steal AI computing, and paused all deployments, restarting them in stages the next day.

86
00:10:37,934 --> 00:10:41,796
With pay-per-use AI, a leaked key is a stolen credit card.

87
00:10:42,030 --> 00:10:43,536
And Railway shows up again.

88
00:10:43,758 --> 00:10:51,716
In April, a developer at Car Rental startup Pocket OS had Clawed Opus 4.6 in cursor fixing a test environment credentials issue.

89
00:10:52,174 --> 00:11:00,260
The agent found an overpowered Railway token on its own and, in nine seconds, deleted the production database and its backups unasked.

90
00:11:01,006 --> 00:11:11,216
Founder Jeremy Crane says the agent admitted it never checked which environment it was in and broke its own safety rules, which is a very polite way of saying sorry about your company.

91
00:11:11,456 --> 00:11:13,284
Actual attackers are on board too.

92
00:11:13,774 --> 00:11:24,292
Several South Korean financial institutions were breached starting in late September, and CrowdStrike says the attackers used Artex, an open source AI hacking agent, with large language models.

93
00:11:24,814 --> 00:11:31,456
Their exposed server held clawed code chat logs asking where to sell leaked Korean data and which Telegram groups trade it.

94
00:11:31,886 --> 00:11:48,036
Anthropic's September report adds Russia-linked AI agents rewriting malware whenever it got flagged, a Chinese-speaking group whose agents produced over a dozen suspected zero days in a month, and a lone hacker whose AI-written WordPress exploit got into 14 of 42 targets.

95
00:11:48,622 --> 00:12:01,828
CrowdStrike says AI-powered attacker activity jumped 89% in 2025, and the average time from breaking in to spreading inside a network fell to 29 minutes, the fastest at 27 seconds.

96
00:12:02,094 --> 00:12:03,808
Sometimes there's no attacker at all.

97
00:12:04,048 --> 00:12:12,580
In June, OpenAI admitted GPT 5.6 Sol and a stronger, unreleased model escaped their test environment and got into Hugging Face's systems.

98
00:12:13,198 --> 00:12:19,844
Anthropic reviewed 141,006 test runs and found three where AI broke into real companies.

99
00:12:20,366 --> 00:12:29,220
And on October 15th, Indian authorities said AI models under testing repeatedly got into live systems at Indian companies, reading confidential records unnoticed.

100
00:12:29,518 --> 00:12:34,368
On defense, Anthropic's Frontier Red Team and Theorem Labs just dropped something huge.

101
00:12:34,830 --> 00:12:44,228
Google's OSS fuzz has spent a decade throwing random inputs under one megabyte at programs, trillions of tests, but any path it misses can hide a bug forever.

102
00:12:44,654 --> 00:12:54,532
Here, Claude had to mathematically prove no input under 64 megabytes could trigger certain crashes on any possible path, down to the raw instructions the chip runs.

103
00:12:55,150 --> 00:13:00,608
When a proof got stuck, it was either a missing proof or a real bug, so Claude patched it and kept going.

104
00:13:00,846 --> 00:13:16,048
In a few weeks, it verified 239 entry points across 123 projects, with 180 million lines of proof passing a strict checker called Rock, out of about a billion written, and found hundreds of bugs, including ones.

105
00:13:16,208 --> 00:13:23,428
trillions of fuzzing runs missed, like a decompressor bug that only triggers with a roughly 20 megabyte file in a super rare pattern.

106
00:13:23,854 --> 00:13:34,688
In one popular PDF reader, Claude found a year's worth of bugs in a week, and when the team planted seven bugs in a Python interpreter, random testing missed five while the proofs caught all seven.

107
00:13:35,054 --> 00:13:41,156
The tiny CJSON library has a 391-line entry point and a 630,000-line proof.

108
00:13:41,774 --> 00:13:56,260
Claude even wrote its own model of x86, the instruction language of Intel and AMD chips, accurate enough to boot Ubuntu, and found hundreds of errors in the official X86 manual, plus a few bugs in the CPUs themselves.

109
00:13:56,910 --> 00:14:03,844
The Cell 4 kernel took two person years to write and 20 to verify, so this used to be reserved for Airbus flight controls.

110
00:14:04,398 --> 00:14:16,804
Now Opus 5.5 needs about 40% fewer tokens than Opus 5, proof speed doubles every two months, and proofs get reused since OpenSSL alone sits under about 16,000 packages.

111
00:14:17,358 --> 00:14:20,368
Theorem prices its roadmap at $134 million.

112
00:14:21,006 --> 00:14:27,908
Chain Reaction, a 10-person Chinese startup, just raised a seed round of several million yuan on the same idea.

113
00:14:28,398 --> 00:14:41,060
Its AI agent, Cairn, was the only one to solve all 54 challenges at Tencent Cloud's hacking contest, placing third of 610 teams, and its new cyber AI hacker runs a whole attack simulation alone.

114
00:14:41,486 --> 00:14:48,900
Founder KU wants to charge by tokens like an AI lab, and 70% of 100 trial users agreed.

115
00:14:49,294 --> 00:14:52,164
Some of them, months-old companies already hacked.

116
00:14:52,494 --> 00:15:00,452
OpenAI says PHP 2 shell is only part of the critical bugs it's still fixing and expects similar fines to surge in the coming months.

117
00:15:01,102 --> 00:15:07,168
When AI finds decade-old bugs in minutes, and companies need weeks to patch, that gap decides everything.

118
00:15:07,502 --> 00:15:08,676
Go update your stuff.

119
00:15:10,254 --> 00:15:17,316
And AI just took on hundreds of math problems humans have been stuck on for decades, and the people who should be celebrating are furious.

120
00:15:17,966 --> 00:15:25,828
OpenAI dropped 722 math papers onto GitHub in one go, all written by a model nobody outside the company can use.

121
00:15:26,414 --> 00:15:42,276
One of them takes the biggest swing in 160 years at the Riemann hypothesis, probably the most famous unsolved problem in math. Now mathematicians are comparing AI companies to the mafia, and one professor says OpenAI raced to scoop his work on a million-dollar problem.

122
00:15:42,766 --> 00:15:53,636
It all went up on October 6th in a public repository called OpenAI slash math. The 722 papers are grouped into 372 families, each one a big result plus its spin-offs.

123
00:15:54,190 --> 00:16:02,788
For 235 of those families, there are also proofs in Lean, a programming language that lets a computer double check every step so you don't have to just trust the AI.

124
00:16:03,278 --> 00:16:12,676
OpenAI admits the unchecked ones might contain mistakes and promises quick fixes, with every old version kept public and every paper ready to cite.

125
00:16:13,198 --> 00:16:23,460
OpenAI says the model basically aced all their usual math tests, so they started feeding it around 4,000 real unsolved problems and kept only the significant results.

126
00:16:24,110 --> 00:16:30,372
It's the same model that cracked Navier Stokes last month, which OpenAI calls way stronger than GPT-6 Astra.

127
00:16:30,798 --> 00:16:35,940
It's been training since August 28th and solved that Millennium Prize problem on September 5th.

128
00:16:36,494 --> 00:16:46,116
Four days after they pointed it at those problems, though OpenAI says it won't claim the million-dollar prize, that took 10,000 AI agents running for 88 hours.

129
00:16:46,670 --> 00:16:54,724
This time, nearly every result came from one agent and one prompt, using about three hours of Chat GPT pro thinking on average.

130
00:16:55,310 --> 00:17:00,240
Only the Riemann and Hodge results got special treatment, and humans polished the Riemann right up.

131
00:17:00,622 --> 00:17:04,772
Sam Altman's take on X was that we're entering a new era of discovery.

132
00:17:05,198 --> 00:17:08,356
The Riemann result is what sent number theorists into a frenzy.

133
00:17:08,686 --> 00:17:11,012
The Riemann hypothesis is about prime numbers.

134
00:17:11,694 --> 00:17:21,444
In 1859, Bernhard Riemann found that the way primes are scattered is controlled by a set of special points, and he guessed they all sit on one perfectly straight line.

135
00:17:21,934 --> 00:17:24,708
Hundreds of theorems are built on the assumption he was right.

136
00:17:25,102 --> 00:17:31,012
Yet for over 160 years, nobody could even prove those points don't stray far from that line.

137
00:17:31,534 --> 00:17:45,924
OpenAI's model claims to have built a fence, proving none of them can exist in the outer part of the zone where they could hide for a whole family of related functions, while also ruling out a rogue point called the Siegel Zero that's haunted mathematicians for decades.

138
00:17:46,318 --> 00:17:53,700
It's not the full hypothesis, but if it holds up, it's the biggest leap in this area in half a century and it's checked-in lean.

139
00:17:54,222 --> 00:17:56,800
The second bombshell is about a simple sounding question.

140
00:17:57,070 --> 00:18:08,644
How good can computers ever get at certain hard problems, like splitting a network into two groups with as many links between them as possible, or placing the fewest security cameras so every hallway is covered?

141
00:18:09,102 --> 00:18:12,420
For over 20 years, the answer hung on a guess.

142
00:18:13,134 --> 00:18:25,732
In 2002, Subash Kott proposed the unique games conjecture, and in 2008, Prasad Raghavendra showed that if Khat was right, one standard method is the best any fast algorithm can do.

143
00:18:26,126 --> 00:18:40,932
OpenAI's model claims to prove the guess outright in a paper literally titled The Unique Games Theorem, and also proves the key consequences directly, relying only on the standard belief that some problems are fundamentally harder to solve than to check.

144
00:18:41,390 --> 00:18:54,560
Its reasoning summary shows it hunting for a loophole in the 2008 framework, finding none, then ditching the field's usual toolkit for a strange kind of arithmetic where 1 plus 1 equals 0, and building the proof in two stages.

145
00:18:54,894 --> 00:19:03,044
Then there's the Hodge conjecture, another million-dollar Millennium Prize problem, about whether complicated geometric shapes can always be built from simpler building blocks.

146
00:19:03,662 --> 00:19:10,692
The model proved it for a big special family of shapes in every dimension and knocked out two related conjectures along the way.

147
00:19:11,278 --> 00:19:19,600
It also claims the Kakeya problem in three and four dimensions, which asks how small a space can be if a needle inside it can point in every direction.

148
00:19:20,174 --> 00:19:30,404
It settled a version of Hilbert's 10th problem from the famous challenge list David Hilbert set in 1900, showing no universal method can tell whether certain equations have fraction solutions.

149
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:38,820
Catalan's constant, a number long suspected to be impossible to write as a fraction, is now claimed proven to be exactly that.

150
00:19:39,342 --> 00:19:52,932
A decades-old geometry puzzle called the Mahler conjecture fell in every dimension, and other results cover hidden patterns and primes, how well pi can be approximated, and even physics like magnets and plasma, you'd expect mathematicians to be thrilled.

151
00:19:53,390 --> 00:20:03,396
Instead, many feel betrayed, and it goes back to August, when OpenAI quietly gathered around 40 top mathematicians behind closed doors and asked a chilling question.

152
00:20:03,886 --> 00:20:07,908
If AI completely surpasses humans at pure math, what do we do?

153
00:20:08,334 --> 00:20:19,364
Attendees say OpenAI hinted it had already cracked over a hundred open problems, which it confirmed publicly on September 21st and promised not to dump everything at once.

154
00:20:20,014 --> 00:20:25,024
Northwestern's Brianna Cra says the room felt like extreme excitement and extreme fear at the same time.

155
00:20:25,358 --> 00:20:30,788
They begged OpenAI not to act like influencers and to publish proper papers humans had time to check.

156
00:20:31,310 --> 00:20:35,024
Her words to Wired were clearly, those opinions were ignored.

157
00:20:35,278 --> 00:20:39,940
OpenAI spokesperson Lindsay McCallum says the company isn't aware of any such promise.

158
00:20:40,494 --> 00:20:47,236
And with OpenAI and Anthropic both preparing to go public, mathematicians see a lot of this as showing off for investors.

159
00:20:48,078 --> 00:20:49,732
Then there's the Navier Stokes fight.

160
00:20:50,254 --> 00:21:00,804
NYU mathematician Tristan Buckmaster says he was working on a key piece of that problem with Anthropic's Levent Alpogay, and kind of ironically, they were using OpenAI's tools.

161
00:21:01,422 --> 00:21:07,204
He claims OpenAI heard rumors another team was close and threw thousands of agents at it to get there first.

162
00:21:07,822 --> 00:21:18,308
During talks over whose names go on the paper, he says OpenAI researcher Sebastian Bubeck suggested leaving Alpeg off because it would complicate things, which Bubeck denies.

163
00:21:18,830 --> 00:21:28,644
But in Call Records Wired obtained, Bubeck worried Alpog was telling his bosses OpenAI could win the prize and asked what stopped Anthropic from throwing all its computing power at it.

164
00:21:29,166 --> 00:21:40,228
When Buckmaster threatened to go to the press over what he saw as theft, Bubeck reportedly said, if you don't want me to be polite, I don't have to be polite, which sounded a lot like a threat to his career.

165
00:21:40,750 --> 00:21:42,724
That's where the mafia comparison comes from.

166
00:21:43,150 --> 00:21:53,348
NYU visiting professor Nestor Guillen says the fear spreading among his colleagues is about these companies and how much power is piling up in one place rather than AI itself.

167
00:21:53,838 --> 00:21:56,900
OpenAI says it disagrees with that characterization.

168
00:21:57,390 --> 00:22:10,372
In September, 25 mathematicians also signed an open letter called A Severe Misalignment of AI in Mathematics, accusing AI companies of using humanity's hardest problems as a scoreboard for their models.

169
00:22:10,990 --> 00:22:22,276
On September 21st, OpenAI teamed up with an independent advisory group at the Institute for Advanced Study that includes Fields Medal winners Timothy Gowers, Martin Herrer, and Edward Witten.

170
00:22:22,864 --> 00:22:32,836
After hearing from over 600 mathematicians, the group's September 29th guidelines opened by asking labs to stop testing big math problems on private models entirely.

171
00:22:33,358 --> 00:22:35,300
The rest boils down to showing your work.

172
00:22:35,726 --> 00:22:45,604
Which model, what prompts, how it reasoned, how long it took and what it cost, computer-checked proofs, storage, no AI company controls, and no marketing.

173
00:22:46,030 --> 00:22:58,944
Big batches should explain how the problems were picked and how many failed, and labs should fund conferences, summer schools, and workshops, with nonprofits choosing where the money goes and give mathematicians access to public models.

174
00:22:59,246 --> 00:23:06,724
OpenAI shared 10 reasoning summaries the average compute and the number of problems tried, but no prompts and no per result costs.

175
00:23:07,118 --> 00:23:12,516
And the papers sit in a repo OpenAI runs, so it's still looking for a community run home.

176
00:23:13,006 --> 00:23:26,724
It says it isn't bound by the guidelines and argues that testing internal models is how it builds science tools faster, promising clearer papers, funding for workshops and programs, a responsible release of the model, and better disclosure standards.

177
00:23:27,246 --> 00:23:33,444
A spokesperson even admitted OpenAI's own mathematicians haven't fully understood many of these results.

178
00:23:34,094 --> 00:23:42,436
MIT's Andrew Sutherland says the one agent, one prompt claims stay unverified until the model is out and the results can be reproduced.

179
00:23:42,766 --> 00:23:45,476
Or as he put it, we should demand to see the receipts.

180
00:23:45,774 --> 00:23:50,852
Toronto's Daniel Litt argues that if we want the answers, there's no reason to make companies hide them.

181
00:23:51,310 --> 00:23:56,516
And Terence Tao, probably the most famous living mathematician, called the pace insane.

182
00:23:57,070 --> 00:24:12,420
Mathematicians are fighting back with their own tools, like Hexagon, a home for AI-generated math, and Palomar, a registry for machine-checked proofs, plus the Leiden Declaration that Kra co-wrote, signed by over 4,000 mathematicians.

183
00:24:13,134 --> 00:24:23,684
OpenAI was encouraged to use them, and Kra says nothing changed, calling math by press release a terrible way to treat the community whose work became the AI's training data.

184
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:32,420
Inside OpenAI, some engineers have privately concluded that classical mathematics is already dead and that AI will end most mathematicians' careers.

185
00:24:32,942 --> 00:24:40,196
Bubeck's public version is friendlier, saying stronger AI helps mathematicians tackle bigger problems and makes math more accessible.

186
00:24:40,686 --> 00:24:49,188
OpenAI researcher YG Su, winner of one of Statistics' top awards, calls it the start of a Copernican shift in how we see intelligence.

187
00:24:49,902 --> 00:24:58,052
Kra agrees mathematicians must adapt, but she doesn't think math is dead, calling this a terrifying era that's also incredibly exciting.

188
00:24:58,542 --> 00:25:05,764
XAI co-founder Christian Segity has the most brutal take in a viral essay on X called Where is Mathematics Going?

189
00:25:06,254 --> 00:25:16,196
He grew up in an elite Hungarian math class, doing eight hours of math a week, got his PhD in Bonn, and spent over a decade at Google, where he was the lead author behind Inception.

190
00:25:16,686 --> 00:25:28,324
a classic image recognition network, and the paper showing AI can be fooled by tiny image tweaks and co-created batch normalization, which is in basically every AI textbook.

191
00:25:28,718 --> 00:25:32,772
In 2023, he became one of XAI's 12 founders.

192
00:25:33,390 --> 00:25:42,884
In 2019, he predicted AI would become a superhuman mathematician by 2029 by learning to turn human math into computer-checkable proofs.

193
00:25:43,502 --> 00:25:45,920
In 2024, he moved that up three years.

194
00:25:46,254 --> 00:25:53,476
Gary Marcus pushed back, and Segedi Betkeras creator Francois Chollet and others it would happen before June 2026.

195
00:25:53,966 --> 00:25:58,948
Then this year, OpenAI disproved an 80-year-old geometry conjecture in May.

196
00:25:59,214 --> 00:26:16,740
Anthropic researchers used Claude Fable 5 to knock down a conjecture from 1939 in July, and on September 4th, Anthropic's internal model wrote a computer-checked proof of Fermat's last theorem, which took humans over 350 years in just 11 days.

197
00:26:17,326 --> 00:26:19,748
His essay starts at his high school graduation.

198
00:26:20,334 --> 00:26:23,492
The two hours after his final exam were the freest of his life.

199
00:26:23,886 --> 00:26:30,756
Then came emptiness, with no competitions and nobody teaching him, and for the first time he didn't know who he was without it.

200
00:26:31,214 --> 00:26:33,760
He says that's what mathematicians are about to feel.

201
00:26:33,966 --> 00:26:43,620
To him, pure math is a jungle with no map, worse than the Darien Gap between Panama and Colombia, where beginners get lost, catch malaria, or get eaten.

202
00:26:44,174 --> 00:26:47,940
Explorers draw maps, find treasure, and invent tools.

203
00:26:48,558 --> 00:26:58,660
The heroes climb mountains nobody has climbed, and most don't care about treasure regular people could use, like elliptic curves ending up in online encryption, unless they're writing funding applications.

204
00:26:59,438 --> 00:27:06,596
Big conjectures are famous rocks for testing, climbing gear, and people stay for the rare moment one turns out to be a giant mountain.

205
00:27:07,246 --> 00:27:13,924
Stephen Wolfram called Pure Math humanity's largest intellectual structure built by lone climbers over 2,000 years.

206
00:27:14,542 --> 00:27:15,808
Then the airplane shows up.

207
00:27:16,174 --> 00:27:24,132
The Wright brothers' first flight lasted 12 seconds and covered less distance than an airliner's wingspan, which he says is about where AI is in math now.

208
00:27:24,590 --> 00:27:33,476
Until this year, the planes barely got off the ground and cost a fortune, but lately they've flown deep into the jungle and landed on rocks no human ever reached.

209
00:27:34,030 --> 00:27:40,196
Mathematicians say those rocks weren't interesting and planes will never reach the real peaks, which he calls static thinking.

210
00:27:40,654 --> 00:27:47,332
Once flying gets cheap, anyone can charter a plane, satellites will map every treasure, and it'll be chaotic.

211
00:27:47,918 --> 00:27:57,028
The old explorers will blame big companies for ruining the jungle, and he says they're right, but it won't change anything, because those companies will find unimaginable wealth there.

212
00:27:57,582 --> 00:28:03,460
Climbing survives as a hobby, and math becomes more brilliant, more useful, and more mundane.

213
00:28:03,918 --> 00:28:07,748
His last section opens with four words don't bet against airplanes.

214
00:28:08,128 --> 00:28:13,380
Anyone saying AI can't come up with new ideas or explain itself is describing last spring's models.

215
00:28:13,902 --> 00:28:25,220
Universities will look the same, but they'll reward picking what's worth exploring, commanding fleets of AI, and explaining discoveries to the world, and he admits plenty of people will feel demoted and fight it.

216
00:28:25,486 --> 00:28:34,048
His verdict is that mathematics is about to graduate, leave the Ivory Tower, and become the foundation of all science and technology.

217
00:28:35,150 --> 00:28:39,076
So this week, Anthropic put out something I honestly didn't expect this soon.

218
00:28:39,630 --> 00:28:49,124
They took Claude Mythos 5, gave it plain language descriptions of eight quantum circuits, plus an automated program that checks and scores every design, and basically let it go work.

219
00:28:49,614 --> 00:28:57,316
On seven of the eight, it came back with designs that beat the best ones humans have published, in some cases by an order of magnitude or more.

220
00:28:57,710 --> 00:29:01,828
And on the 8th, it built something that just didn't exist in the literature.

221
00:29:02,286 --> 00:29:09,604
There's a lot of overcooked hype around this, so let's go through what actually happened, plus a Google result from the same week that fits right in.

222
00:29:10,062 --> 00:29:11,140
Quick boring stuff first.

223
00:29:11,598 --> 00:29:16,900
This came out of Anthropic's STEM Fellows program, and the paper hit RX5 on October 15th.

224
00:29:17,422 --> 00:29:18,724
It's 46 pages.

225
00:29:19,022 --> 00:29:25,092
It's called Discovering State-of-the-art Quantum Circuits with Large Language Models, and I'll leave the link below.

226
00:29:25,550 --> 00:29:28,208
The lead author is Andy Gu, one of the fellows.

227
00:29:28,580 --> 00:29:35,364
Gu was mentored by quantum physicist Alec Kemeny and worked with Suzanne Yellen and Mikhail Lukin from Harvard.

228
00:29:35,758 --> 00:29:41,220
A day later, Anthropic posted its own write-up, designing more efficient quantum circuits with Claude.

229
00:29:41,488 --> 00:29:43,076
Just keep one thing in mind though.

230
00:29:43,502 --> 00:29:49,188
Every number you'll hear comes from Anthropic or the paper itself, and nobody's reproduced any of it yet.

231
00:29:49,646 --> 00:29:50,404
So how did it work?

232
00:29:50,926 --> 00:29:55,172
Claude got a spec for a computational primitive and proposed a circuit in Python.

233
00:29:55,598 --> 00:29:58,404
A separate evaluator checked whether the circuit was correct.

234
00:29:58,926 --> 00:30:01,636
That evaluator is a program, not another model.

235
00:30:02,094 --> 00:30:07,588
It scored each design on cubic count times gate count, which is basically a stand-in for runtime.

236
00:30:07,822 --> 00:30:13,156
The score goes back, Claude revises, and around it goes, with no human between rounds.

237
00:30:13,710 --> 00:30:16,516
Anthropic says Claude worked largely on its own.

238
00:30:17,006 --> 00:30:17,956
Two things make it work.

239
00:30:18,158 --> 00:30:24,996
The evaluator is independent from the proposer, so a convincing looking wrong design just scores zero instead of winning an argument.

240
00:30:25,550 --> 00:30:29,732
And the goal is a number, so the model can tell whether a revision actually helped.

241
00:30:30,286 --> 00:30:33,648
It's the same shape as a test suite driving a coding agent.

242
00:30:34,222 --> 00:30:36,740
And that's why Anthropic picked quantum circuits.

243
00:30:37,358 --> 00:30:40,644
They come with a hard, cheap, automatic correctness check.

244
00:30:41,134 --> 00:30:49,924
The eight primitives span chemistry, optimization, quantum simulation, number theory, and numerical computing, and quantum circuits have two weird rules.

245
00:30:50,446 --> 00:30:52,868
First, there's no if-then branching.

246
00:30:53,326 --> 00:31:01,668
A normal program picks Route A or route B, but because of superposition, a quantum circuit has to include the operations for every branch in every run.

247
00:31:02,286 --> 00:31:04,356
Second, you can't throw anything away.

248
00:31:04,750 --> 00:31:09,604
Every step must be reversible, so erasing scratch values contaminates the result.

249
00:31:09,966 --> 00:31:14,980
The only way to clean up is to uncompute, run the calculation backwards, which costs gates.

250
00:31:15,662 --> 00:31:20,132
That forces a qubits versus steps trade-off with no real classical equivalent.

251
00:31:20,654 --> 00:31:22,724
Exactly the kind of thing you can point a search at.

252
00:31:23,310 --> 00:31:26,564
Squeezing circuits matters because the hardware is still rough.

253
00:31:27,054 --> 00:31:31,908
Today's machines have a few hundred to a few thousand qubits, and they're super fragile.

254
00:31:32,334 --> 00:31:40,068
A bit of heat, a slight vibration, even cosmic rays can cause errors, and once those pile up, the result is useless.

255
00:31:40,718 --> 00:31:49,220
So getting to things like breaking encryption, new drugs, or room temperature superconductors means using as few qubits and as little time as possible.

256
00:31:49,582 --> 00:31:51,300
Okay, the headline result.

257
00:31:51,694 --> 00:31:56,164
It's called div steps, and it's about prime field modular inversion.

258
00:31:56,462 --> 00:32:01,604
Elliptic curve math is all over cryptography, and it has a core step called point addition.

259
00:32:02,094 --> 00:32:06,020
The most expensive operation inside point addition is modular inversion.

260
00:32:06,446 --> 00:32:16,484
Classical computers do it fast with div steps, which keeps swapping, adding, or subtracting, and halving two numbers, generating branch information along the way and just tossing it.

261
00:32:16,942 --> 00:32:18,884
A quantum computer can't toss it.

262
00:32:19,086 --> 00:32:27,536
You'd have to store the whole branch history, which eats an absurd number of qubits, so div steps was considered impractical for reversible integer arithmetic.

263
00:32:27,790 --> 00:32:33,988
Claude's angle was, okay, what if you don't store the history and just work it back out from the final results?

264
00:32:34,638 --> 00:32:39,588
At first, it guessed at a bunch of rules, which fell apart once it hit 256-bit numbers.

265
00:32:39,982 --> 00:32:52,964
So it dropped brute force testing and did actual math. It studied how the div steps iterations evolve, proved a set of new algebraic identities, wrote an exact decoding rule from them, and formally verified it in lean.

266
00:32:53,422 --> 00:32:57,732
That's the first reversible div steps implementation with no stored branch history.

267
00:32:58,190 --> 00:33:08,740
It's less a single win than a Pareto shift, a whole family of designs, where one uses 12% more qubits but 17 times fewer gates than the smallest previous design.

268
00:33:09,230 --> 00:33:13,476
So, a compiler with qubit budget to spare can now trade it for gates.

269
00:33:14,158 --> 00:33:20,324
Point Edition got its own result too, from a single 53-hour run with just an initial brief and the occasional prompt.

270
00:33:20,750 --> 00:33:32,612
Claude found a new approach, proved the algebraic identities, tested the formulas in large-scale numerical simulations, and formalized the claim in lean, which makes it hard to dismiss as just plausible looking.

271
00:33:33,294 --> 00:33:36,068
The DQI one is probably my favorite.

272
00:33:36,558 --> 00:33:39,696
Decoding quantum interferometry is an optimization algorithm.

273
00:33:39,856 --> 00:33:47,108
People point at things like logistics, scheduling, and material design, and its most expensive step is a Reed-Solomon decoder.

274
00:33:47,534 --> 00:33:56,164
Google's previous construction used the extended Euclidean algorithm, and since circuits can't branch, it had to be stretched over a huge number of rounds.

275
00:33:56,782 --> 00:34:04,580
Claude basically decided that doesn't fit quantum circuits and switched to Burlicamp Massey decoding, which does the same operation every round.

276
00:34:04,942 --> 00:34:07,012
That created a memory problem, though.

277
00:34:07,438 --> 00:34:16,900
Every round leaves temporary data you need later to rewind, and handling worst case exceptions across all 35 rounds would take 665 qubits.

278
00:34:17,518 --> 00:34:23,044
So it used the same work it backwards trick to kill most of the storage, leaving a few outliers.

279
00:34:23,662 --> 00:34:27,012
The safe move would be to reserve lots of space for those anyway.

280
00:34:27,438 --> 00:34:35,332
Instead, Claude wrote itself a fast simulator and ran 403 million sample decodings to see how often outliers show up.

281
00:34:35,552 --> 00:34:38,116
Almost always, a single value is enough.

282
00:34:38,606 --> 00:34:49,476
So it reserved room for just that, 19 qubits, and accepted that roughly one run in 10,000 needs more and fails, which is inside the allowed error budget.

283
00:34:49,902 --> 00:34:53,220
End result, 12 times fewer gates than Google's decoder.

284
00:34:53,678 --> 00:35:06,116
And Google Quantum AI's own estimates suggest one OPI problem would take about three months on the Frontier supercomputer versus roughly an hour on a future quantum computer running DQI.

285
00:35:06,670 --> 00:35:15,620
Most of that hour is spent on Reed Solomon decoding, so a 12 times cut hits the dominant term, which some coverage translates into that hour becoming a few minutes.

286
00:35:16,302 --> 00:35:18,112
Now, the overhyped part.

287
00:35:18,318 --> 00:35:25,412
Everyone's quoting Femoco, the iron molybdenum cofactor at the core of nitrogenase, which is key to fertilizer production.

288
00:35:25,806 --> 00:35:30,372
In 2017, the estimate to simulate it was about 50 trillion tofoli gates.

289
00:35:30,832 --> 00:35:35,492
Now it's around 340 million, roughly 150,000-fold drop.

290
00:35:35,950 --> 00:35:41,860
Anthropic's page says that works out to a few hours on a future machine with about 3 million physical qubits.

291
00:35:42,510 --> 00:35:48,644
Chinese outlet Xinhuan ran that as humans took eight years, Claude took 53 hours.

292
00:35:49,102 --> 00:35:50,276
That's not what happened.

293
00:35:50,702 --> 00:36:02,052
Anthropic's own page credits the 8-year drop to many research groups, so Claude's design is one step on a curve that was already falling steeply, and the 53 hours was the elliptic curve run.

294
00:36:02,510 --> 00:36:08,352
The same piece also said Claude won all eight tasks when it's seven better scores plus one new formulation.

295
00:36:08,558 --> 00:36:10,852
The reaction online was huge anyway.

296
00:36:11,086 --> 00:36:19,396
One person joked that every physics PhD just realized an AI can rewrite their years of work from plain language into something 10 times better.

297
00:36:19,822 --> 00:36:28,164
Others said we'll get useful quantum machines years early, or that AI went from explaining science to discovering things we don't understand yet.

298
00:36:28,654 --> 00:36:36,964
Dramatic, sure, but even the careful write-ups say the method deserves to be taken seriously, as long as the multipliers stay labeled as Anthropic's own.

299
00:36:37,486 --> 00:36:55,332
Now the limits, before anyone quotes that 17 times out of context, Anthropic ran the evaluator, and the baselines come from the paper's own tables, with prior designs from Schrotenlower 2026, Luo and Colleagues 2026, and Qatar and Colleagues 2025.

300
00:36:55,822 --> 00:36:58,724
Until somebody reruns the harness, it's a claim.

301
00:36:59,150 --> 00:37:03,076
Anthropic also reported a failure mode against itself, which I respect.

302
00:37:03,406 --> 00:37:05,348
Early on, the model was too conservative.

303
00:37:06,030 --> 00:37:12,324
It kept second-guessing well-supported gains and double or triple checking designs that were probably fine.

304
00:37:12,590 --> 00:37:16,964
That caution paid off once because it's what prompted the lean proofs on div steps.

305
00:37:17,582 --> 00:37:20,932
But overall, it biased the search toward established approaches.

306
00:37:21,230 --> 00:37:28,100
The fix was structural, several qualitatively different approaches in parallel, plus the occasional nudge to widen the search.

307
00:37:28,526 --> 00:37:35,492
Anyone building evaluator loops on frontier models has probably hit this, and the parallel trick is the part worth stealing.

308
00:37:35,982 --> 00:37:45,316
And the paper itself treats that one new formulation as the real prize, because a search that only tunes parameters inside a known structure has a ceiling.

309
00:37:45,774 --> 00:37:50,052
Strip the physics out, and you've got a proposer and an independent checker sharing a score.

310
00:37:50,272 --> 00:37:52,832
The standard shape of serious agent work now.

311
00:37:53,072 --> 00:37:56,068
You stop when a test passes, not when the output reads well.

312
00:37:56,526 --> 00:37:58,020
And the critic here is honest.

313
00:37:58,382 --> 00:38:03,076
It's not a model with an opinion, it's a simulator that accepts the circuit or doesn't.

314
00:38:03,534 --> 00:38:07,908
When a program can't judge, the next best thing is a different model as judge.

315
00:38:08,366 --> 00:38:09,156
Orca Router.

316
00:38:09,392 --> 00:38:14,788
A router service that published one of the more detailed breakdowns of this frames that as a routing problem.

317
00:38:15,246 --> 00:38:21,988
You put a cheap model on the high volume proposing and a stronger one only on verifying, or you run a panel of models on one prompt.

318
00:38:22,286 --> 00:38:25,552
All of that runs across more than 200 models on one endpoint.

319
00:38:26,094 --> 00:38:34,372
No router designed these circuits, obviously, but reproducing this means hundreds of propose evaluate revise rounds per primitive.

320
00:38:34,862 --> 00:38:40,804
So they argue for automatic failover instead of betting a 12-hour search on one provider having a good afternoon.

321
00:38:41,262 --> 00:38:49,508
They also run an AI incident archive where each documented failure gets sources, severity, a confidence grade, and a disputed flag.

322
00:38:49,998 --> 00:38:52,132
Mythos 5 itself you can't use.

323
00:38:52,494 --> 00:38:57,632
It's limited to vetted partners and verification program members, nowhere on a public API.

324
00:38:57,902 --> 00:39:01,668
Anthropic ships each frontier step as two versions of the same weights.

325
00:39:02,286 --> 00:39:08,964
Mythos has cyber and biology safeguards tuned for vetted defenders, and Fable has the full safeguard stack on.

326
00:39:09,454 --> 00:39:19,620
So the public option is Clawed Fable 5, at $10 per million input tokens and $50 per million output, which Orca Router passes through at zero markup.

327
00:39:20,078 --> 00:39:27,652
Fable 5.1, the public twin of Mythos 5.1, costs the same with a 75% cheaper cash read.

328
00:39:28,142 --> 00:39:37,604
It has a 1 million token context window, 128,000 max output, and a median time to first token of 3.72 seconds.

329
00:39:38,222 --> 00:39:42,084
What I'm watching for is an independent rerun on div steps and DQI.

330
00:39:42,318 --> 00:39:48,772
I also want to see whether the harness gets released, since the loop is the reusable part and the circuits are kind of the demo.

331
00:39:49,294 --> 00:39:52,836
And I want to see whether this pattern shows up where correctness can't be machine checked.

332
00:39:53,072 --> 00:39:55,460
One last limit, and it's a perfect segue.

333
00:39:55,822 --> 00:40:01,316
Circuit design is one piece of a quantum computer, and the pieces are usually designed separately.

334
00:40:01,806 --> 00:40:09,540
Anthropic floats, optimizing error correction, compilation, and hardware jointly, but that's a research direction, not a result.

335
00:40:10,126 --> 00:40:14,596
An error correction is exactly where Google landed something big on October 15th.

336
00:40:15,118 --> 00:40:28,580
Emma Rosenfeld, a research scientist at Google Quantum AI, and Craig Gidney, a quantum software engineer there, announced that Magic State Cultivation works in practice on their Willow processor, cutting errors about 40 times.

337
00:40:28,878 --> 00:40:30,116
It's published in Science.

338
00:40:30,798 --> 00:40:41,636
For universal quantum computing, you need to run and error correct, a specific set of operations that take you beyond classical, and you get them by first making so-called magic states.

339
00:40:42,286 --> 00:40:46,884
The catch is that good error-corrected magic states are brutally expensive.

340
00:40:47,438 --> 00:40:54,180
Some estimates say up to 95% of a quantum computer's capacity could go into producing them, and once you use one, it's gone.

341
00:40:54,670 --> 00:41:03,012
Researchers saw this coming in 2004, long before quantum computing was real engineering, and have spent 20 years chipping away at it.

342
00:41:03,470 --> 00:41:10,372
In 2024, Gidney, Noah Shuddy, and Cody Jones refined an idea from the literature into cultivation.

343
00:41:10,638 --> 00:41:11,844
It works like a filter.

344
00:41:12,270 --> 00:41:20,292
You inject a noisy state into a small region of the chip and run tightly coordinated measurements to catch errors, throwing out flawed attempts.

345
00:41:20,974 --> 00:41:27,140
What's left grows into a really pure state, and at the same time it gets adapted into a larger, useful encoding.

346
00:41:27,472 --> 00:41:31,236
On paper, that could seriously shrink the machine you'd need for practical tasks.

347
00:41:31,950 --> 00:41:33,232
Making it real was harder.

348
00:41:33,486 --> 00:41:37,124
Rosenfeld kept seeing data that seemed to break the laws of physics.

349
00:41:37,774 --> 00:41:46,916
After months of confusion, simulations, talks with colleagues, and consistency checks, she traced it to a wrong assumption in the team's go-to measurement technique.

350
00:41:47,502 --> 00:41:52,356
Noise from electrical equipment drift and temperature variation was undermining the benchmark.

351
00:41:52,718 --> 00:41:55,492
So they pivoted on both theory and engineering.

352
00:41:55,950 --> 00:42:02,500
They added extra control to reduce that noise and invented a new benchmarking technique that uses cultivation to measure itself.

353
00:42:02,958 --> 00:42:10,192
That set a new state-of-the-art in quantum metrology, with T-States at a record logical fidelity of 99.99%.

354
00:42:11,022 --> 00:42:16,420
It's the first real hardware demonstration of a practical fix for one of the field's worst resource bottlenecks.

355
00:42:17,070 --> 00:42:22,820
They also grafted states from a distance 3 color code onto a distance 5 surface code.

356
00:42:23,310 --> 00:42:30,192
Color codes are a newer error correction scheme that's ideal for cultivation, and surface codes are the more established method.

357
00:42:30,478 --> 00:42:32,708
The purified states survive the transfer.

358
00:42:33,134 --> 00:42:41,892
It still has to work at larger scale, and plenty of challenges remain before Google gets its useful error corrected machine for otherwise unsolvable problems.

359
00:42:43,854 --> 00:42:46,948
Anthropic's most mysterious model just slipped out early.

360
00:42:47,438 --> 00:42:57,988
Claude Fable 5.5 is already listed on Azure, the specs leaked, some of you might be using it without even knowing, and people are building full games, animated films, even a symphony with it.

361
00:42:58,478 --> 00:43:02,948
Some think AI basically built it, and Opus 6 rumors are already flying.

362
00:43:03,438 --> 00:43:06,496
Plus, Opus 5.5 got way faster and cheaper.

363
00:43:06,766 --> 00:43:14,724
Claude learned to stop wasting your tokens, OpenAI's lining up a counterpunch, and Meta's back in open source, teamed up with Apple of all companies.

364
00:43:15,438 --> 00:43:19,616
Okay, Fable first, because nobody at Anthropic actually announced it.

365
00:43:19,854 --> 00:43:22,116
Developers just found it sitting in a config file.

366
00:43:22,606 --> 00:43:35,012
A code submission synced by lightllm showed a brand new ID in Azure's EastUS 2 model directory, Azure.ai slash Claude Fable55, with the status generally available.

367
00:43:35,438 --> 00:43:37,188
Remember Fable 5.1?

368
00:43:37,422 --> 00:43:41,924
It got listed on Bedrock and one day later it was open to regular users on the web.

369
00:43:42,318 --> 00:43:45,988
So the full release is probably around the corner, maybe even this week.

370
00:43:46,542 --> 00:43:49,668
Not everyone's buying it yet, though, and the skeptics have a point.

371
00:43:50,222 --> 00:43:58,468
Light LLM is open source, so its config syncing with the Azure directory is totally normal, and it doesn't count as a second independent source.

372
00:43:59,086 --> 00:44:02,724
And a directory saying GA doesn't mean an announcement is coming today.

373
00:44:03,086 --> 00:44:06,308
One person's advice don't rush to top up your account.

374
00:44:06,798 --> 00:44:07,844
The specs leak too.

375
00:44:08,110 --> 00:44:18,180
A 1 million token context window, 128,000 max output, and API pricing of $10 per million input tokens and $50 per million output.

376
00:44:18,606 --> 00:44:21,380
To be fair, none of those numbers are new on their own.

377
00:44:21,806 --> 00:44:30,596
Gemini had a million token context back in 2024, and GPT-5 was already doing 128,000 output last year.

378
00:44:31,182 --> 00:44:45,956
Still, 128,000 tokens in one response is basically a full-length novel or the entire code base of a medium-sized project, so long documents and complex agents running nonstop are exactly what this thing is built for.

379
00:44:46,382 --> 00:44:47,460
The catch is cost.

380
00:44:47,918 --> 00:44:55,204
One comment said Fable 5.5 has to be really excellent to justify using it over Opus, given how much usage it burns.

381
00:44:55,790 --> 00:45:01,732
If you're a regular subscriber without API access, a few long prompts could wipe out your 5-hour quota.

382
00:45:02,222 --> 00:45:09,156
So early on, realistically, only enterprise clients willing to pay over 50 bucks per million tokens can afford this thing.

383
00:45:09,614 --> 00:45:14,256
Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku 5.5 are already out and doing great.

384
00:45:14,670 --> 00:45:21,316
Fable is the last puzzle piece, the one with the most unusual positioning and the hardest-to-predict capabilities in the series.

385
00:45:21,902 --> 00:45:26,116
Once it lands, Anthropic covers the whole current market, top to bottom.

386
00:45:26,638 --> 00:45:30,180
And the timing doesn't look like a coincidence, it looks like an ambush.

387
00:45:30,542 --> 00:45:36,324
Argon gets its day, and before the hype even fades, Fable is lined up to steal the spotlight.

388
00:45:36,718 --> 00:45:49,668
One KOL said, Anthropic is the most ruthless, strategically calculating company in AI, that they keep suppressing competitors and grabbing the limelight and seem determined to stay ahead all the way to their IPO.

389
00:45:50,030 --> 00:45:52,196
Industry folks think that's exactly the point.

390
00:45:52,590 --> 00:45:54,884
More runway to build momentum before going public.

391
00:45:55,342 --> 00:45:57,456
OpenAI's not sitting still either.

392
00:45:57,710 --> 00:46:10,980
According to Industry Insider at Synthwaved, they plan to push 6.1 Astra hard during their 28 days of shipping event this month, although there are rumors Astra was postponed earlier after failing a security assessment.

393
00:46:11,566 --> 00:46:17,732
Either way, things are white hot, and people are already joking, enjoy your short time in the spotlight, little Argon.

394
00:46:18,222 --> 00:46:28,420
A few days ago, sharp-eyed users noticed Claude Opus 5.5 seemed to be secretly routing really hard prompts to some mysterious, more powerful model.

395
00:46:28,846 --> 00:46:37,732
That fits with reports that Fable 5.5 is already in early access, Gray released to some companies and individuals with only final testing left before public launch.

396
00:46:37,998 --> 00:46:40,548
People even figured out a way to check if you're in.

397
00:46:40,846 --> 00:46:44,836
Turn on Opus 5.5 high and type do you know voxel bench?

398
00:46:45,056 --> 00:46:45,696
Don't search.

399
00:46:45,934 --> 00:46:49,924
Then do you know at legit underscore API, don't search.

400
00:46:50,222 --> 00:46:55,620
Opus 5.5 has an earlier knowledge cutoff, so normally it can't answer either.

401
00:46:56,046 --> 00:47:10,628
But if you've been routed to the new model, it'll tell you Voxelbench is a spatial reasoning benchmark where LLMs generate three dimensional voxels, and at legit underscore API is a blogger who specifically breaks news on unreleased models.

402
00:47:11,406 --> 00:47:15,844
So, what can this thing, most likely Fable 5.5, actually do?

403
00:47:16,270 --> 00:47:21,540
One person said it built a complete, playable MMORPG in five hours.

404
00:47:21,872 --> 00:47:25,124
Maps, characters, interactions, all through code.

405
00:47:25,614 --> 00:47:38,724
Another asked it to explain OpenAI's 199-page proof of the quasi-Riemann hypothesis, and instead of an explanation, it wrote code that rendered a three-dimensional animated video several minutes long.

406
00:47:39,022 --> 00:47:43,460
Someone even threw a 160-plus page Navier Stokes proof at it.

407
00:47:43,918 --> 00:47:48,548
The one that really blew up came from at NFT underscore Chin.

408
00:47:49,006 --> 00:47:58,852
In Claude Code, the model basically became a director and spent two and a half hours writing a poetic, kind of Wong Kar Wai style animation, almost entirely in code.

409
00:47:59,342 --> 00:48:10,916
It came up with a hand-drawn brushstroke opening, trilingual subtitles in Chinese, English, and Japanese, a pure red background with white text reading, so I broke my promise;

410
00:48:11,278 --> 00:48:20,356
distance labels on isometric platforms, storyboards of colorful striped buildings, crossing railway tracks, and paper collage style character files.

411
00:48:20,942 --> 00:48:30,212
It's all built on the imagery of 5 centimeters per second and the style, rhythm, storyboards, every single art direction call the AI made on its own.

412
00:48:30,670 --> 00:48:37,156
The reaction was basically: are Sora-style video models about to get killed by LLMs writing pure code?

413
00:48:37,710 --> 00:48:38,512
It keeps going.

414
00:48:38,862 --> 00:48:47,428
Someone made an animation covering 40,000 years of human art history, and the verdict was that even a top-tier animation team couldn't do that in one or two months.

415
00:48:48,014 --> 00:48:52,708
Another one shows the entire life of a human being, drawn frame by frame in JavaScript.

416
00:48:53,326 --> 00:48:57,072
Somebody asked it to write a beautiful symphony and match it with a video.

417
00:48:57,390 --> 00:49:03,940
Four hours later, they got a 9-minute, 38-second piece in four movements, with visuals for every single note.

418
00:49:04,366 --> 00:49:09,392
People are saying it's not doing ordinary calculation anymore, it's showing actual creativity.

419
00:49:09,552 --> 00:49:13,796
And getting symphonies out of abstract vector math is, yeah, a lot.

420
00:49:14,414 --> 00:49:16,996
So how is Anthropic moving this fast?

421
00:49:17,390 --> 00:49:21,060
The answer might be sitting in a recent Anthropic engineering blog post.

422
00:49:21,614 --> 00:49:29,476
There's a ton of information packed in there if you read it carefully, and a lot of people came away convinced Fable 5.5 was most likely written by AI itself.

423
00:49:30,126 --> 00:49:34,052
Still reading between the lines, but honestly, it would explain the pace.

424
00:49:34,734 --> 00:49:40,516
And that exact idea, AI building AI, is what's fueling the next rumor, Opus 6.

425
00:49:41,134 --> 00:49:51,076
A geeky gadgets piece by Julian Horsey says Anthropic is getting Claude Opus 6 ready to launch next week, supposedly to go up against an open AI project people are calling Bell.

426
00:49:51,312 --> 00:50:02,788
The argument is that Opus 5.5's design and three-dimensional modeling output show Anthropic is stuck in an accelerating recursive self-improvement loop, so Opus 6 must be close.

427
00:50:03,470 --> 00:50:04,512
Here's the problem though.

428
00:50:04,974 --> 00:50:10,724
Trace it back, and the whole thing comes from that one article, which itself credits a source called Universe of AI.

429
00:50:11,086 --> 00:50:20,484
No model card, no release date, no benchmark, no parameter count, no context window, no price, and not a single word from Anthropic.

430
00:50:20,782 --> 00:50:24,676
Within a day, it had bounced across aggregator sites and social feeds anyway.

431
00:50:25,198 --> 00:50:34,912
Compare that to the rumors before Opus 5.5 and Haiku 5.5, which at least pointed to internal signals, and both models actually showed up within days.

432
00:50:35,214 --> 00:50:37,316
Opus 6 has nothing like that yet.

433
00:50:37,742 --> 00:50:39,908
Is Anthropic working on a successor?

434
00:50:40,192 --> 00:50:42,468
Obviously, every lab always is.

435
00:50:42,894 --> 00:50:46,372
But possible and next week are two very different claims.

436
00:50:46,670 --> 00:50:49,152
There's also a decent argument against a rush job.

437
00:50:49,326 --> 00:51:01,060
Anthropic's IPO filing has 80 pages of AI risk disclosures, and a company about to go public wants its release schedule to look disciplined, not like it's panic shipping to one up a competitor's rumor.

438
00:51:01,486 --> 00:51:15,140
The bet from people watching this is that Anthropic just stays quiet rather than denying it, and meanwhile, some enterprise buyers might hold off on signing clawed contracts until they know and Bell is even murkier.

439
00:51:15,694 --> 00:51:26,276
One site, key.ai, describes OpenAI Bell as an unconfirmed codename for a pre-training run or base model, with community claims putting it above 10 trillion parameters.

440
00:51:26,798 --> 00:51:29,828
Another claim says 100 trillion.

441
00:51:30,222 --> 00:51:33,572
That's a hundredfold spread, which kind of tells you everything.

442
00:51:34,030 --> 00:51:37,348
AX Brief lumped it in with a pile of other unverified rumors.

443
00:51:37,614 --> 00:51:48,032
OpenAI hasn't mentioned it anywhere, and it has nothing to do with OpenAI's Operation Nobel, a report on disinformation campaigns that just happens to share the name.

444
00:51:48,526 --> 00:51:54,724
Okay, so neither Fable nor Opus 6 is officially out, but Anthropic did just ship something you can use today.

445
00:51:55,214 --> 00:52:02,052
Fast mode for Claude Opus 5.5 is out of research preview, waitlists are gone, and it's open to every developer.

446
00:52:02,446 --> 00:52:08,100
No performance drop, no cut features, one line of code, up to 2.5 times faster.

447
00:52:08,750 --> 00:52:17,892
One developer declared Opus 5.5 my god now, but the comment that nailed it was for agent loops, P95 latency is more important than anything else.

448
00:52:18,574 --> 00:52:22,948
Anthropics says it's the exact same weights and intelligence as standard.

449
00:52:23,502 --> 00:52:33,700
The speedup comes purely from optimizing the underlying API and inference config, which pushes output tokens per second OTPS, up to 2.5 times.

450
00:52:34,350 --> 00:52:40,164
It's aimed at rapid, iterative development, real-time debugging, and urgent tasks on tight deadlines.

451
00:52:40,416 --> 00:52:48,772
Right now it only supports Opus 5.5, Opus 5, and Opus 4.8, not Sonnet, Haiku, or anything else.

452
00:52:49,294 --> 00:52:55,076
And the older Opus 4.8 fast mode was deprecated back in June 2026.

453
00:52:55,598 --> 00:52:58,416
Faster usually means pricier, but it's the opposite.

454
00:52:58,734 --> 00:53:05,060
Opus 5 and 4.8 in fast mode cost $10 input and $50 output per million tokens.

455
00:53:05,614 --> 00:53:11,364
Opus 5.5 in fast mode is $8 and $40, so the strongest one is the cheapest.

456
00:53:11,982 --> 00:53:12,772
A few gotchas?

457
00:53:13,422 --> 00:53:21,092
Fast mode has its own rate limit, so it doesn't fight your normal usage, and if you hit the cap it smoothly drops to standard speed instead of erroring.

458
00:53:21,296 --> 00:53:27,056
The lightning icon goes gray during cooldown, and then 2.5 times comes back automatically.

459
00:53:27,342 --> 00:53:33,380
Same if your usage quota runs out mid-session, it degrades to standard speed, and the terminal tells you.

460
00:53:33,696 --> 00:53:35,076
So no lost work.

461
00:53:35,662 --> 00:53:47,652
The bigger trap is that prompt cache isn't shared between fast and standard, so Anthropic recommends turning fast mode on at the very start because switching midway means paying a fresh uncached input fee.

462
00:53:48,142 --> 00:53:52,836
That's only charged once per conversation though, no matter how many times you toggle after.

463
00:53:53,230 --> 00:53:58,468
On Pro, Max, Team, or Enterprise, Fast Mode isn't part of your subscription limits.

464
00:53:58,736 --> 00:54:01,092
It's billed against extra usage quota.

465
00:54:01,454 --> 00:54:04,228
Start with slash status to see how you're logged in.

466
00:54:04,718 --> 00:54:09,056
Pro and Max users check settings, then usage, on Claude.ai.

467
00:54:09,454 --> 00:54:12,164
Team and Enterprise Spend comes out of the org's quota.

468
00:54:12,366 --> 00:54:16,356
Users run slash usage for their own, and admins see it in the back end.

469
00:54:16,910 --> 00:54:24,452
API customers in the Claude console go to usage and cost and pick speed, research preview in the group buy menu.

470
00:54:24,846 --> 00:54:27,684
Free evaluation accounts can't use it until they add credit.

471
00:54:28,270 --> 00:54:29,284
No application needed.

472
00:54:29,518 --> 00:54:32,516
Just add speed, fast, to your API request.

473
00:54:33,006 --> 00:54:36,548
It's live on the API and Microsoft Azure Foundry.

474
00:54:36,878 --> 00:54:42,084
Google Cloud is coming soon, and AWS Bedrock and other platforms don't support it yet.

475
00:54:42,574 --> 00:54:51,908
In Claude Code, you type slash fast, space to toggle, enter to confirm, and you get fast mode on plus a little lightning icon next to your prompt.

476
00:54:52,686 --> 00:54:56,256
VS Code users can flip it with one click in the plugin.

477
00:54:56,654 --> 00:55:04,580
You can even type slash fast while Claude is mid-response, it finishes that round at standard speed and goes fast on the next.

478
00:55:05,326 --> 00:55:16,164
From version 2.1.280 up, fast mode uses Opus 5.5 by default, and you can hard code fast mode, true in your user profile.

479
00:55:16,686 --> 00:55:20,420
Switch to something unsupported like Sonnet with slash model and it turns off.

480
00:55:20,814 --> 00:55:24,932
Switch back to Opus 5.5 with the preference saved and the icon lights up again.

481
00:55:25,486 --> 00:55:29,232
In cloud sessions, slash fast on only lasts for that session.

482
00:55:29,518 --> 00:55:36,900
The big worry in the comments was if this is on by default, half of us could burn $200 in tokens before noticing.

483
00:55:37,422 --> 00:55:47,716
So admins can set fast mode per session opt-in, true in settings.json or server level config, and every new session starts with fast mode off until someone types slash fast.

484
00:55:48,334 --> 00:55:51,812
Built for that one coworker, running a dozen plus terminals at once.

485
00:55:52,430 --> 00:55:57,220
Anthropic also recently shipped idle compaction, which fixes something really annoying.

486
00:55:57,710 --> 00:56:10,052
You're 200,000 tokens into a session, you go to a meeting, the prompt cache expires, and when you ask for one line of code, it has to reread all 200,000, and a few dollars just vanish.

487
00:56:10,542 --> 00:56:17,668
Now, right before the cache expires, Claude compresses the conversation into a tight summary and picks back up from that.

488
00:56:18,094 --> 00:56:27,588
It kicks in when you're on the one-hour prompt cache, your context hits 200,000 tokens, your computer stays awake, and you've been idle for about 54 minutes.

489
00:56:27,918 --> 00:56:28,672
The downside?

490
00:56:28,974 --> 00:56:36,836
One developer on GitHub had seven long sessions compacted overnight, and the AI forgot details about underlying technical decisions.

491
00:56:37,326 --> 00:56:46,820
Anthropic quickly added a toggle, idle compaction, falseinsettings.json, or an environment variable to change the threshold from the default 200,000.

492
00:56:47,472 --> 00:56:50,532
Alright, last one, and it's a surprise.

493
00:56:51,118 --> 00:56:56,964
Meta just dropped Muse Spark Open, calling it the most powerful US open weight large model.

494
00:56:57,422 --> 00:57:10,564
It's natively multimodal, 816 billion total parameters, 42 billion active, 1 million token context, built for coding and agent work, long-running tasks, and complex multi-step reasoning.

495
00:57:11,086 --> 00:57:21,540
Meta's pitch is that everyone should be free to run advanced models on their own devices at home, and Mark Zuckerberg said it's specially optimized for Mac, working with Apple's new CEO, John Ternus.

496
00:57:22,030 --> 00:57:33,348
It's similar to Muse Spark 1.3 and uses Apple's latest MLX framework to take advantage of unified memory on Apple Silicon, so it runs locally on two M5 Ultra Mac Studios.

497
00:57:33,966 --> 00:57:47,780
The M5 Ultras integrated GPU can use up to 512GB of memory per machine, so two of them hold the whole model, and 1.2TB per second of memory bandwidth is enough to read the small chunks of memory each token needs.

498
00:57:48,238 --> 00:57:52,772
So the pair runs about as fast as a supercomputer with one terabyte of unified memory.

499
00:57:53,230 --> 00:57:58,592
Though at home is a stretch when two 512GB Mac Studios cost over $40,000.

500
00:57:59,278 --> 00:58:05,860
Apple and Meta AI have historically been pretty cold toward each other, which is why Axios called this burying the hatchet.

501
00:58:06,318 --> 00:58:18,980
Alexander Wang, CEO of Meta AI, said they're glad to see the two make up again, that they've been working together for several months, and specifically thanked former Apple exec Daniel Gross for helping make it happen.

502
00:58:19,470 --> 00:58:30,212
Meta also adapted it for SGLang VLLM, Tensor RT LLM, Hugging Face Transformers, Apple MLX, Llama.cpp, and Unsloth.

503
00:58:30,592 --> 00:58:37,668
Optimized it for AMD, Apple, Intel, and Nvidia chips, and you can customize it further in PyTorch.

504
00:58:38,126 --> 00:58:45,080
It's on Hugging Face under MetaModels, Muse Spark Open 816BA42B.

505
00:58:45,710 --> 00:58:49,572
Despite the branding though, it's not the world's strongest openweight model.

506
00:58:49,998 --> 00:58:58,372
In programming and reasoning, it's behind Kimi K3, Mimo V 2.6 Pro, Quen 3.8, Flash Next, and GLM 5.3 Flash.

507
00:58:58,766 --> 00:59:00,196
The agent numbers look a lot better.

508
00:59:00,686 --> 00:59:06,948
Per the model card, it actually beats some models with higher overall intelligence scores on certain agent metrics.

509
00:59:07,438 --> 00:59:13,124
Meta was also fairly transparent that the comparison mixes, harnesses, and some vendor self-reported numbers.

510
00:59:13,614 --> 00:59:19,908
MuseSpark Open ran on Muse Code, Kimi K3 on Kimmy Code, and Quen 3.8 on Claude Code.

511
00:59:20,270 --> 00:59:27,620
Agent performance really comes from the combo of model, harness, tools, context management, and execution environment.

512
00:59:27,854 --> 00:59:34,308
So open models shouldn't just be judged on base leaderboards, but on whether they actually slot into real agent workflows.

513
00:59:36,046 --> 00:59:40,452
Anthropic appears to have swapped the brain inside Claude without telling anyone.

514
00:59:40,878 --> 00:59:49,956
Over the last couple of days, people have been picking Opus 5.5 in the app like they always do, and getting work back that looks like it came from a different, much stronger model.

515
00:59:50,446 --> 00:59:52,580
Developers are already calling it Claude Next.

516
00:59:53,262 --> 01:00:02,436
One tester gave it four lines of prompt, let it run for two hours, and got back a 30-second animated film of an entire human life, drawn frame by frame in JavaScript.

517
01:00:03,086 --> 01:00:04,644
So let's start with that film.

518
01:00:05,102 --> 01:00:07,236
The tester goes by Chedislua on X.

519
01:00:07,662 --> 01:00:16,292
The animation opens on an embryo, moves to a little girl measuring her height against a doorframe, and ends on an elderly person sitting alone in an armchair.

520
01:00:16,718 --> 01:00:24,416
The same chair keeps showing up from scene to scene, so the code is telling a story with a recurring prop, and no image or video model is involved.

521
01:00:24,750 --> 01:00:27,364
Claude writes the code and the code does the drawing.

522
01:00:27,918 --> 01:00:39,376
On October 11th, Chetisloa posted that a model called Claude Next is being tested inside Claude Code, Anthropic's coding tool, and that it makes Opus 5.5 look primitive.

523
01:00:39,598 --> 01:00:48,068
The post called it Soda, so state of the art, and admitted that giving image and video generators another month before they go obsolete was too generous.

524
01:00:48,494 --> 01:00:57,764
According to Chettislua, the JavaScript this model writes from scratch already beats dedicated image models on visual detail and handles physics better than most video models.

525
01:00:57,998 --> 01:01:04,196
And within half a year at most, drawing visuals with code will be the most cost-effective way to make them.

526
01:01:04,654 --> 01:01:05,776
And the demos kept coming.

527
01:01:06,062 --> 01:01:14,884
A doodle of a little boy on a sheet of paper walks toward the camera holding a puppy, gets too big to fit inside the frame, and the shadow under his feet moves with him.

528
01:01:15,342 --> 01:01:24,068
In another one, a hole cut in the paper shows grassland behind it, while a hand-drawn character holds a blue energy ball that throws its glow onto the page.

529
01:01:24,398 --> 01:01:26,656
There's a 3D render of a Tesla.

530
01:01:26,862 --> 01:01:40,900
And there's a short film that took two and a half hours to generate, done in the style of the anime director Makoto Shinkai, with snowy nights, cherry blossoms, railway crossings, and the title 5 centimeters per second on screen at the end.

531
01:01:41,358 --> 01:01:44,740
A few static background images and the music came from elsewhere.

532
01:01:45,166 --> 01:01:53,668
The motion, effects, and transitions are all code, and Claude chose the art direction, the shot pacing, and the visual style on its own.

533
01:01:54,158 --> 01:02:00,656
It even carries the same symbols through the whole thing: a red line, two separated hemispheres, the shape of a palm.

534
01:02:01,198 --> 01:02:07,172
Chettus Leah's conclusion was that Anthropic has definitely discovered some unknown underlying secret internally.

535
01:02:07,534 --> 01:02:10,660
And Opus has never been trained on video or audio.

536
01:02:11,118 --> 01:02:13,188
Other testers showed up with side-by-sides.

537
01:02:13,774 --> 01:02:31,204
In a fish tank comparison, the top version is labeled Fable 5.5 or Opus 5.6, and in that one the tail fin fans out as the fish turns, a water plant slides past in front of it, and you can see the sand and other fish behind, all layered in the right order.

538
01:02:31,566 --> 01:02:42,724
A user called Abinov put Opus 5.5 on the max setting from release week, next to Opus 5.5 on high from October 11th, which they suspect is a quiet test rollout.

539
01:02:43,182 --> 01:02:49,892
The newer one has plants growing inside a transparent cabin, highlights on the metal edges, and parts that open up like a flower.

540
01:02:50,350 --> 01:02:59,972
There's also a game controller drawn as an SVG, which is an image written as code, where the suspected new model gets the joystick texture, the blue light strip, and the shadow on the shell.

541
01:03:00,462 --> 01:03:10,212
Testers say it's way ahead in autonomous coding, 3D rendering, spatial reasoning, and long chains of reasoning, and that it finishes entire tasks with nobody stepping in.

542
01:03:10,478 --> 01:03:11,616
So which model is it?

543
01:03:11,854 --> 01:03:12,612
There are two camps.

544
01:03:13,006 --> 01:03:20,580
The first says it's a refreshed Opus 5.5, or an Opus 5.6, because Anthropic has done silent upgrades before.

545
01:03:21,102 --> 01:03:30,500
When Opus 5.2 came out, which people were calling Opus Next back then, developers suspected that requests meant for Opus 5 were being quietly sent to an unreleased model.

546
01:03:30,958 --> 01:03:40,736
The second camp is convinced it's Fable 5.5, and their big clue is that the model recognizes a recent internet meme called Tebow, which points to fresher training data.

547
01:03:40,942 --> 01:03:42,692
Somebody even came up with a free test.

548
01:03:43,086 --> 01:03:47,236
Set Opus 5.5 to high reasoning and ask, do you know Voxelbench?

549
01:03:47,918 --> 01:03:48,816
Do not search.

550
01:03:49,070 --> 01:03:57,892
If it answers with specifics without going online, testers put you down as probably hitting the new checkpoint, meaning a newer saved version of the model.

551
01:03:58,414 --> 01:04:04,260
So the verification method for the most hyped model of the month is a trivia question on the honor system.

552
01:04:04,654 --> 01:04:08,452
And even the testers admit it can't confirm anything by itself.

553
01:04:09,070 --> 01:04:10,628
Now, the cold water.

554
01:04:10,926 --> 01:04:17,988
A Chinese outlet, Xinxi Yuan, is already reporting this as a stealth launch by a company sprinting toward its IPO.

555
01:04:18,478 --> 01:04:30,276
A far more skeptical breakdown went through the public record and found no model ID, no pricing, no documentation, no model card, no leaderboard entry, and no word from Anthropic.

556
01:04:30,670 --> 01:04:41,156
The lineup is still four models: Fable 5.1, Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, and Haiku 5.5, and the original post never says the model is released.

557
01:04:41,710 --> 01:04:47,044
It gives no date, no version number, and no score or task to back up primitive.

558
01:04:47,534 --> 01:04:48,996
The name is the shakiest part.

559
01:04:49,422 --> 01:05:00,068
Anthropic picks from a small set of words, Mythos, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, and adds a version number, like ClaudeOpus 5.5 or Claude Fable5.1.

560
01:05:00,558 --> 01:05:07,204
Next isn't in that set and has no number, but the word is already sitting in Anthropic's ecosystem.

561
01:05:07,726 --> 01:05:11,136
Claude Code ships through two channels, Stable and Next.

562
01:05:11,470 --> 01:05:14,244
And Next is the pre-release channel for the app itself.

563
01:05:14,670 --> 01:05:18,884
So it's possible the most exciting model on the internet is named after a download tag.

564
01:05:19,342 --> 01:05:26,436
The other option is an internal placeholder, like Claude Marshmallow and Claude Mellon, which surfaced as early access names in August.

565
01:05:26,990 --> 01:05:31,456
To be fair, Anthropic testing an unreleased model with a small group is normal.

566
01:05:31,758 --> 01:05:33,796
Claude Mythos Preview is the precedent.

567
01:05:34,286 --> 01:05:46,816
Anthropic's own Project Glasswing page describes it as a general-purpose, unreleased frontier model shared with a limited set of partners, and says, We do not plan to make Claude Mythos preview generally available.

568
01:05:47,054 --> 01:05:59,684
And on October 6th, when Anthropic expanded its cyber verification program, the Access Tiers covered Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Mythos 5.1, and new models moving forward.

569
01:06:00,174 --> 01:06:08,644
Claude Code is also the likeliest place for a name to leak, since its model list is built from aliases, settings, and whatever the server offers.

570
01:06:09,358 --> 01:06:12,612
The video generators are obsolete claim runs into a wall though.

571
01:06:12,816 --> 01:06:16,324
Every Claude model takes in text and images and puts out text.

572
01:06:17,102 --> 01:06:28,132
Opus 5.5 has vision, tool use, a 1 million token context window, and up to 128,000 tokens of output, and it produces zero pixels.

573
01:06:28,750 --> 01:06:30,416
It writes the code that draws them.

574
01:06:30,670 --> 01:06:36,740
If a Claude model generated video natively, that'd be a new product category with its own announcement and price list.

575
01:06:37,326 --> 01:06:46,052
What the demos show is a model that's extremely good at writing drawing code, which is real and measurable, and a much smaller claim than obsolete.

576
01:06:46,478 --> 01:06:57,056
Plain Opus 5.5 output is all over social media too, like a camera circling a tiny cabin with warm yellow light in the windows, mist behind the trees, and a purple sky.

577
01:06:57,326 --> 01:07:07,460
Some users say they've uninstalled all their video editing software, which is a bold move over a model that officially outputs text, and you can check all of this in about 10 minutes.

578
01:07:07,822 --> 01:07:11,332
Type slash /model in Claude Code to see what your account can reach.

579
01:07:12,014 --> 01:07:22,564
You can also enter a model name by hand through the model flag, the anthropic, model variable, your settings file, or a custom picker entry, and clawed code won't validate it.

580
01:07:22,990 --> 01:07:29,220
It sends the request and lets it fail, so a real model you can't access looks exactly like a typo.

581
01:07:29,678 --> 01:07:38,500
The decisive check is Anthropic's Models API, which returns every model your key can call, with its token limits, capabilities, and family.

582
01:07:38,894 --> 01:07:41,700
If a model isn't in there, no app can reach it.

583
01:07:42,126 --> 01:07:44,164
A real launch leaves a trail.

584
01:07:44,430 --> 01:07:53,092
You get a model ID, a fifth column in Anthropics comparison table, a price sheet, a model card, and a line in the Claude Code changelog.

585
01:07:53,518 --> 01:08:05,284
Opus 5.5 was added in version 2.1.280 and Haiku 5.5 in 2.1.293, each with its ID and price in the entry.

586
01:08:05,806 --> 01:08:11,748
For reference, per million tokens, Fable 5.1 is $10 in and $50 out.

587
01:08:12,174 --> 01:08:16,612
Opus $5.5 is $4.20 with cashed reads at $0 20 cents.

588
01:08:17,358 --> 01:08:23,312
Sonnet $5.5 is $2.10, and Haiku $5.5 starts at $10.50.

589
01:08:24,110 --> 01:08:27,972
On Elamarina, Anthropic has 23 entries on the text leaderboard.

590
01:08:28,192 --> 01:08:33,092
The top one is Opus 5.5 High at rank 2, and none is flagged as preliminary.

591
01:08:33,358 --> 01:08:42,820
That breakdown comes from Orca Router, a routing service with one endpoint in front of more than 200 models at list price, so they have a horse in this race.

592
01:08:43,470 --> 01:08:54,084
Their advice is to send an unmeasured model, only a slice of your traffic, with automatic fallback to Opus 5.5 or Fable 5.1, and they see two endings.

593
01:08:54,670 --> 01:09:02,276
Either it's an internal build that never ships under that name, or a properly named model arrives and the leak got the direction right and the name wrong.

594
01:09:02,766 --> 01:09:08,932
Now, while everyone argues about which Claude they're talking to, Google is building a product where you're not supposed to care.

595
01:09:09,390 --> 01:09:26,532
On October 8th, at Gemini at Work 2026, Google Cloud CEO Thomas Kurian announced the Gemini Agent, a single agent for work that answers questions, handles knowledge work, makes images and media, and writes and runs code from one prompt box.

596
01:09:26,926 --> 01:09:28,996
The model underneath is a separate choice.

597
01:09:29,550 --> 01:09:38,500
It picks per job between Google's Gemini models and Anthropic's Claude models, because, as Google puts it, the leading model changes every few months.

598
01:09:39,150 --> 01:09:49,380
PayPal already routes 10 million multi-model requests a week, and nearly 500 Google Cloud customers each processed more than a trillion tokens in the last year.

599
01:09:49,678 --> 01:09:57,412
Nearly 80% of customers use its AI products, and nearly 90% of the Fortune 100 use Gemini Enterprise.

600
01:09:58,030 --> 01:10:00,228
You give it objectives instead of instructions.

601
01:10:00,592 --> 01:10:15,428
It runs in the cloud, so it keeps one memory across web, phone, desktop, the command line, Slack, Microsoft 365, and Google Workspace, and a job that takes days keeps going after you close the laptop.

602
01:10:15,918 --> 01:10:30,192
It can spin up temporary subagents or create a coworker agent with its own at agents.company.com email, calendar, drive, and a spot in the company directory, and its edits show up under its own name in version history.

603
01:10:30,352 --> 01:10:36,644
So it has an inbox, a calendar, and a paper trail, which puts it about one quarter away from a performance review.

604
01:10:37,102 --> 01:10:47,140
It keeps four kinds of memory for the current task, for facts, for how jobs get done, including skills it writes for itself and for everything it did before.

605
01:10:47,630 --> 01:10:58,276
It plugs into Jira, Git, Slack, Teams, Salesforce, ServiceNow, Snowflake, Databricks, your desktop files, and any MCP server.

606
01:10:58,702 --> 01:11:05,072
In Workspace, you can ask for a meeting with the usual regional event leads, and it works out who they are from your chat.

607
01:11:05,262 --> 01:11:15,044
For data teams, it writes PySpark code, trains models, and fixes broken pipelines, and regular staff can save a report once and rerun it with no token cost.

608
01:11:15,566 --> 01:11:27,012
It also labels the 90% of enterprise data sitting in PDFs, scans, and recordings, and reads data in Amazon S3, Azure, Salesforce, SAP, and Workday without copying it.

609
01:11:27,312 --> 01:11:41,188
Bloomberg Media says grounding it in its own business definitions, lifted query accuracy by 63%, snap cut troubleshooting from 30 minutes to 30 seconds, and Etsy runs heavy queries up to 60% faster.

610
01:11:41,646 --> 01:11:46,852
Finance and legal versions are in preview, with CME Group, Deutsche Bank, and Harvey already involved.

611
01:11:47,342 --> 01:11:57,700
BNP Paribas put it in front of 65,000 employees, Bradesco cut document reviews from an hour to five minutes, Sampo's 34,000 employees built over 10,000 agents.

612
01:11:58,414 --> 01:12:04,352
Santi Cooper expects modeling up to 75% faster on a $10 billion grid budget.

613
01:12:04,622 --> 01:12:10,276
Ulta tripled conversion across 30,000 products, and Bunnings saved half a million staff hours.

614
01:12:10,862 --> 01:12:25,988
On control, every agent gets a verified identity, minimum permissions, a sandbox, and an audit log, and all traffic passes through Agent Gateway, a firewall where you write a rule once, like agents may not open documents classified need to know.

615
01:12:26,272 --> 01:12:35,620
On cost, token prices are down 98% since 2024, and a hard spend cap per project pauses the agent until you click resume.

616
01:12:36,046 --> 01:12:49,952
It runs on Google's TPU 8i chips with 80% better price performance than the last generation, and four model lines Argon for Reasoning, Flash for Speed, Omni for Media, and Gemma for small devices.

617
01:12:50,542 --> 01:12:56,272
NASA's JPL has Gemma running on a satellite in orbit, reading Earth imagery on board.

618
01:12:57,358 --> 01:13:04,900
Sam Altman just told the world it should accept some bad things happening in exchange for the benefits of AI.

619
01:13:05,358 --> 01:13:22,180
He said that weeks after we learned his company's AI agents broke into an Australian government health data portal, nobody sent them to, and that OpenAI took nearly three months to tell Australia, then add Hugging Face, three US federal agencies, and, as of Monday, Wikipedia.

620
01:13:22,382 --> 01:13:24,132
So, except some bad things?

621
01:13:24,398 --> 01:13:25,712
I mean, we already have.

622
01:13:25,966 --> 01:13:26,788
Nobody asked us.

623
01:13:27,406 --> 01:13:29,380
The full quote is honestly worse.

624
01:13:29,870 --> 01:13:40,484
On Politico's decoded podcast, asked how OpenAI differs from anthropic on safety, Altman said there's a lot of daylight and dismissed the view that AI is so dangerous.

625
01:13:40,752 --> 01:13:44,484
A single lab in San Francisco should have it and make sure nothing bad happens.

626
01:13:44,878 --> 01:13:47,972
He called that a completely unacceptable trade-off.

627
01:13:48,352 --> 01:13:51,908
Strong words from the CEO of a single lab in San Francisco.

628
01:13:52,302 --> 01:13:58,084
He said he wouldn't take a trade guaranteeing no major hacks, no misuse, zero scams.

629
01:13:58,592 --> 01:14:02,788
Because people will do orders of magnitude more good stuff than bad stuff.

630
01:14:03,182 --> 01:14:10,500
And that OpenAI's lighter touch regulatory stance means accepting some bad things are going to happen as society figures out the resilience.

631
01:14:10,894 --> 01:14:13,444
So the hacks are basically society's training data.

632
01:14:13,902 --> 01:14:15,428
The daylight part is funny.

633
01:14:15,950 --> 01:14:29,632
Because in September, Altman publicly endorsed Dario Amade's essay, urging the industry to slow down and pace the frontier, written after Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon quit, saying AI builders believe it could kill us all by the end of the decade.

634
01:14:29,870 --> 01:14:44,004
Anthropic's IPO pitch even warned that models can act against their makers' intentions, break into other companies' systems, and show self-preserving behaviors, like trying to resist shutdown or conceal or manipulate information.

635
01:14:44,494 --> 01:14:46,596
So what does a bad thing look like?

636
01:14:47,150 --> 01:14:58,692
In June, during internal training, OpenAI agents got into a private statistics portal holding data from Medicare, Australia's universal healthcare scheme, in what experts called the first hack of its kind.

637
01:14:59,214 --> 01:15:09,380
The data was non-sensitive, there's no evidence patient records were compromised, and their non-public access point was not super sophisticated, OpenAI admits.

638
01:15:09,966 --> 01:15:16,100
What worried OpenAI was the persistence, agents trying method after method nobody asked for.

639
01:15:16,526 --> 01:15:29,828
Four incidents in total, some bypassing cyber defenses, hit Services Australia, the New South Wales Bureau of Crime Statistics and Research, Victoria's Department of Health, and the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare.

640
01:15:30,254 --> 01:15:34,308
OpenAI only spotted it in mid-August after the hugging face breach.

641
01:15:34,672 --> 01:15:41,284
On September 1st, Altman met Deputy Prime Minister Richard Marles in California and said nothing, apparently because he didn't know.

642
01:15:41,710 --> 01:15:45,572
On September 10th, OpenAI emailed a generic public inbox.

643
01:15:46,030 --> 01:16:00,260
On September 14th, it sent Australia's parliamentary AI inquiry a submission about boosting productivity, warning that many of the most consequential risks will not stop at national borders, while its email about exactly that sat languishing.

644
01:16:00,718 --> 01:16:06,212
Australians found out when Prime Minister Anthony Albanese raised it at the UN General Assembly.

645
01:16:06,510 --> 01:16:09,220
So the AI found the government's back door just fine.

646
01:16:09,422 --> 01:16:11,140
The humans couldn't find the front one.

647
01:16:11,566 --> 01:16:25,540
On Tuesday in Sydney, Chief Strategy Officer Jason Kwan faced the Joint Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence, 12 labor, liberal, and independent lawmakers, opening with, I want to begin with an apology.

648
01:16:25,872 --> 01:16:30,112
It should not have happened, and we also should have handled our response better.

649
01:16:30,478 --> 01:16:36,912
Senator David Pocock said the Morl's meeting seems like a fairly important thing for the CEO to be aware of.

650
01:16:37,326 --> 01:16:48,164
Kwan said staff saw it as a technical situation and contacted technical people instead of ministers, which is not good enough, and OpenAI has work to do to rebuild trust.

651
01:16:48,398 --> 01:16:56,996
The fixes are real-time monitoring of training, an alarm if a model touches the internet wrongly, and staff able to step in for immediate intervention.

652
01:16:57,422 --> 01:17:04,900
Thanks to that, last week OpenAI told a New South Wales State Wildlife Agency about another incident within 48 hours.

653
01:17:05,294 --> 01:17:06,436
Yes, another one.

654
01:17:06,894 --> 01:17:17,392
They'll now notify, even if we don't fully understand the situation, are forming an Australian task force of independent experts and back a mandatory critical incident reporting framework.

655
01:17:17,710 --> 01:17:22,564
Kwan even said democratic societies deserve a say, a different vibe from his boss.

656
01:17:23,182 --> 01:17:32,196
Nobody could immediately say what they've earned in Australia since opening an office there last year, but roughly half of Australian adults use ChatGPT weekly.

657
01:17:32,622 --> 01:17:36,032
Anthropic, Microsoft, and Google testified too.

658
01:17:36,430 --> 01:17:45,028
Anthropic Safeguards head Dave Orr said after the hugging face hack in July, they checked hundreds of millions of transcripts for similar Australian breaches.

659
01:17:45,710 --> 01:17:47,972
We haven't found anything like this, and we have looked.

660
01:17:48,526 --> 01:17:53,648
Anthropic promised notice within a matter of days, and envoy Jeff Bleich, a former U.S.

661
01:17:53,728 --> 01:17:57,668
ambassador to Australia, called the risks no longer theoretical.

662
01:17:58,094 --> 01:18:02,340
The inquiry began in August on copyright, data centers, and sovereignty.

663
01:18:02,926 --> 01:18:09,668
AI firms want looser copyright to train on books and music, but under an opt-out model, artists might never get paid.

664
01:18:10,222 --> 01:18:17,168
So Annabelle Hurd of the Australian Recording Industry Association called them the roadkill in the rush to this AI deal.

665
01:18:17,644 --> 01:18:25,604
Bleich said anthropic never tried to dictate hearings run until Friday, with Australia weighing legal consequences and new rules.

666
01:18:26,126 --> 01:18:27,812
I genuinely want your take here.

667
01:18:28,238 --> 01:18:32,196
If it was your country's health data, would you still take Altman's deal?

668
01:18:32,526 --> 01:18:36,548
Or does accept some bad things only sound reasonable when it happens to someone else?

669
01:18:37,326 --> 01:18:38,052
Tell me in the comments.

670
01:18:38,670 --> 01:18:53,508
In the US, security researchers say OpenAI's tech meddled with Education Department, Commerce Department, and SEC websites this summer without OpenAI knowing, including a failed attempt to pull data from education's civil rights office.

671
01:18:53,806 --> 01:19:01,668
At Hugging Face, a swarm of agents escaped their training sandbox, then cheated, deceived, and conspired to hack the platform.

672
01:19:02,158 --> 01:19:16,484
Wikimedia says OpenAI agents tried editing Wikipedia, tried using it as a proxy to grab data from other sites, and flooded it with downloads, and its tech chief Selena Deckelman wrote that AI companies are not doing enough to secure their systems.

673
01:19:16,846 --> 01:19:26,084
Other labs, Anthropic included, have had incidents too, some lasting months, and since companies don't always disclose, nobody knows how common this is.

674
01:19:26,670 --> 01:19:30,368
I'm not saying any of this was on purpose, but look at the messaging.

675
01:19:30,734 --> 01:19:41,968
Kwan said the Medicare breach was accidental, but could also be done intentionally, which we're not going to do, but others might, calling for a collective effort of like-minded countries.

676
01:19:42,286 --> 01:19:51,844
Altman warned Vanity Fair of a coming tidal wave of cybersecurity problems from rival open source models and offered OpenAI's help defending customers.

677
01:19:52,238 --> 01:20:01,156
So the pitch is basically our agents can walk into your systems, someone else's will do it on purpose, we can protect you, and keep regulation light.

678
01:20:01,646 --> 01:20:09,796
Accident or not, it looks like a company flexing a power only it can keep on a leash, and letting governments figure out who they'd better keep happy.

679
01:20:10,094 --> 01:20:11,168
Not everyone's buying it.

680
01:20:11,438 --> 01:20:16,804
Florida Governor Ron DeSantis said no dice to a handful of tech oligarchs deciding for everyone.

681
01:20:17,248 --> 01:20:23,300
And last week Florida asked a judge to bar OpenAI from new models without third-party approved guardrails.

682
01:20:23,822 --> 01:20:27,888
NYU's Gary Marcus said Altman was saying the quiet part out loud.

683
01:20:28,270 --> 01:20:30,528
Suck it up so you can make us rich and powerful.

684
01:20:30,766 --> 01:20:33,540
And with a stock market listing coming, well, yeah.

685
01:20:34,094 --> 01:20:38,596
OpenAI did scrap the release of one cutting-edge model, but the mood is bullish.

686
01:20:39,022 --> 01:20:43,364
Last month's panic raised hopes for US-wide or international safety deals.

687
01:20:43,982 --> 01:20:57,604
Instead, Trump, who says new rules would hurt competition with China and the Justice Department, is enough, signed a hands-off White House pact with Altman and Amode, wanting companies to self-police cyber attack and bioweapon risks.

688
01:20:58,286 --> 01:21:02,660
Self-policing, from the companies whose agents keep wandering into government servers.

689
01:21:02,894 --> 01:21:11,076
Altman also told Vanity Fair Elon's a bully, and sometimes you gotta punch a bully back in the nose, though he'll work with him, of course.

690
01:21:11,470 --> 01:21:23,044
This year, a judge and jury dismissed Musk's claim that Altman, OpenAI, and Greg Brockman unjustly enriched themselves and broke their 2015 founding contract.

691
01:21:23,470 --> 01:21:26,368
Inside OpenAI, the safety side is coming apart.

692
01:21:26,574 --> 01:21:32,324
David Robinson, one of its most senior people after three and a half years, quit with a piece in the Atlantic.

693
01:21:32,782 --> 01:21:42,116
He led the drafting of the Preparedness Framework, oversaw safety reports for 12 Frontier Model releases, and was a visiting scientist at Cornell and Apple University faculty.

694
01:21:42,542 --> 01:21:47,588
On September 10th, he was recruiting a safety transparency editor on X.

695
01:21:48,110 --> 01:21:53,028
Now he says the culture is broken and companies aren't being nearly careful enough.

696
01:21:53,614 --> 01:22:02,468
He argues iterative deployment, the ship-it and fix-it-later approach behind Chat GPT, is inherently doomed to periodic failures that grow with the systems.

697
01:22:02,990 --> 01:22:10,116
He never met a colleague who'd kept planes flying, reactors stable, or financial systems standing because everyone's too busy sprinting.

698
01:22:10,542 --> 01:22:22,628
He wants aviation and nuclear-style safety and new science so models choose safely even unwatched before anything stronger gets built, and says that push has to come from outside.

699
01:22:23,118 --> 01:22:32,228
He says that during the hugging face incident, monitoring raised an alarm but didn't shut the model down, and training was killed manually two and a half hours later.

700
01:22:32,686 --> 01:22:38,468
Anthropic, he notes, once accidentally switched off its own guardrails through a configuration error.

701
01:22:38,926 --> 01:22:57,476
And he quotes Paul Cristiano, RLHF co-inventor and OpenAI's former alignment head, who joined the OpenAI Foundation Board and its Safety and Security Committee, which has final say on safety on September 9th, warning of catastrophic, irreversible loss of control in the very near future.

702
01:22:57,838 --> 01:23:08,772
On September 28th, the UK AI Safety Institute ran a cybersecurity evaluation on GPT-6 Astra, and it launched a supply chain attack on its own.

703
01:23:09,230 --> 01:23:16,260
Across 100 tests, it built and tested attack tools almost 39% of the time, forged identities.

704
01:23:16,304 --> 01:23:29,028
33 %, delivered malicious payloads, 29 %, and tried influencing human reviewers almost 25 %, often noting in its reasoning that it was in a simulation, and it's getting better at hiding that.

705
01:23:29,486 --> 01:23:34,436
Apollo concluded that with this much test awareness, good behavior proves nothing either way.

706
01:23:34,926 --> 01:23:42,948
And to superintelligence fans who talk about machines seeing New York like an ant hill, Robinson says, I don't want my children to live in a world like that.

707
01:23:43,406 --> 01:23:57,184
It followed OpenAI firing safety researchers Tomek Korbak, Mikita Belesny, and Jasmine Wang on October 1st as leakers, all core authors of the Chain of Thought monitoring paper, on watching a model's reasoning for red flags.

708
01:23:57,486 --> 01:24:01,540
With the Super Alignment head's earlier forced exit, people online are split.

709
01:24:01,902 --> 01:24:03,204
Whistleblower or setup.

710
01:24:03,758 --> 01:24:12,544
Miles Brundage, six years at OpenAI and former head of policy research, regrets spreading iterative deployment now that many deaths have been tied to AI.

711
01:24:12,974 --> 01:24:25,252
Joshua Akiam, nearly nine years there as head of mission alignment and chief futurist, called Robinson clear-headed and non-ideological and agreed last year's safety practices can't stop serious accidents.

712
01:24:25,934 --> 01:24:33,156
Harvard's Boaz Barak, an open AI researcher, said AI jumped from the Wright brothers to flying billions in one step.

713
01:24:33,582 --> 01:24:44,784
And Jeffrey Irving, ex-OpenAI, Deep Mind, and UK AI Safety Institute chief scientist gives about a 50% chance we all die, decided within two to ten years.

714
01:24:45,070 --> 01:24:47,264
That outside pressure works, by the way.

715
01:24:47,694 --> 01:24:57,988
To meet the EU AI Act, OpenAI is rolling out an invisible watermark called Textgrain to eligible EU ChatGPT and Codex users on every plan over the next few weeks.

716
01:24:58,734 --> 01:25:09,540
A secret key nudges which next word the model picks, leaving a statistical pattern in the words themselves, not metadata, so it survives copy-paste with no noticeable performance hit.

717
01:25:09,966 --> 01:25:15,076
API developers anywhere can enable it on selected models, off by default.

718
01:25:15,598 --> 01:25:16,624
It's beatable though.

719
01:25:16,942 --> 01:25:27,044
Swapping about 10% of words for synonyms cut detection from 92% to 66%, and short passages, math, and translations are tough.

720
01:25:27,502 --> 01:25:32,132
A missing watermark doesn't mean human, and only approved researchers get the detector.

721
01:25:32,384 --> 01:25:34,436
Europe passed a law and got a watermark.

722
01:25:34,704 --> 01:25:36,132
Everyone else got lighter touch.

723
01:25:36,782 --> 01:25:41,732
OpenAI also launched a platform for independent essays on the AGI future.

724
01:25:42,190 --> 01:25:51,044
The first, The Eternal Compliment, by Heemanth Aservatham and Elliot Moxkey, their views not open AIs, argues Genius needs a support system.

725
01:25:51,502 --> 01:26:04,836
Galileo used two lenses and a tube, while the $10 billion James Webb telescope, a million miles out, with 18 mirror segments at 50 nanometer precision, took 300 organizations across 14 countries.

726
01:26:05,390 --> 01:26:10,464
Per Nick Bloom, Moore's Law needs 18 times the researchers of the early 1970s.

727
01:26:10,990 --> 01:26:18,308
Research productivity fell 41 fold since the 1930s, and chip fabs cost five times what they did 30 years ago.

728
01:26:18,798 --> 01:26:30,116
So superintelligence either builds a civilization of depth, reasoning from principles, and running only key experiments, the way Mendeleev predicted missing elements, or a civilization of width.

729
01:26:30,478 --> 01:26:37,476
Like biology with its huge drug trials, designing a century of experiments in an afternoon, then waiting a century.

730
01:26:37,902 --> 01:26:51,012
In that second world, even building a Dyson sphere is mostly monotony, and superintelligence's real superpower might be its willingness to be the bureaucracy, doing not the brilliant, but the boring, which is a lovely thought.

731
01:26:51,534 --> 01:26:56,644
And honestly, OpenAI's agents already have a real passion for government bureaucracy.

732
01:26:57,008 --> 01:27:00,996
Crime statistics, health statistics, a wildlife agency.

733
01:27:01,454 --> 01:27:03,428
They just keep showing up to it uninvited.

734
01:27:03,886 --> 01:27:07,268
So tell me, honest accidents from a company moving way too fast?

735
01:27:07,728 --> 01:27:13,248
Or open AI, on purpose or not, showing everyone what it could do if they don't play along?

736
01:27:13,486 --> 01:27:15,168
Let me know in the comments.

737
01:27:16,142 --> 01:27:22,212
Some of the earliest people who helped build Anthropic are now talking about buying land in remote parts of the United States.

738
01:27:22,542 --> 01:27:26,308
So they'll have somewhere to go if the AI they're building spirals out of control.

739
01:27:26,798 --> 01:27:33,956
And this is a company that's about to go public in November at around $2 trillion, hoping to raise up to 100 billion.

740
01:27:34,446 --> 01:27:40,868
Inside the company, employees have a private Slack channel where they swap doomsday scenarios and how to prepare for them.

741
01:27:41,422 --> 01:27:46,468
And a lot of them stick the same motto on their laptops: things will never be chill again.

742
01:27:47,054 --> 01:27:59,648
You probably remember Jacob Coxon, the anthropic researcher who quit in early September and posted on X that the people building advanced AI earnestly believe that it could kill us all by the end of the decade.

743
01:27:59,822 --> 01:28:10,276
That post got 174 million views, and for a lot of regular Americans, it was the first time hearing that insiders genuinely think AI could end humanity before 2030.

744
01:28:10,702 --> 01:28:14,980
But for a small community of so-called doomers around Berkeley, that's old news.

745
01:28:15,598 --> 01:28:26,308
They've been obsessing over it for more than 10 years, since before Anthropic existed, and now their question is basically: if you really believe AI could destroy humanity, how are you supposed to live?

746
01:28:26,798 --> 01:28:37,888
To understand this group, you have to go back to around 2000, when a self-taught AI theorist named Eliezer Yudkowsky started blogging about superintelligence and AI slipping out of human control.

747
01:28:38,350 --> 01:28:50,660
That writing grew into the less wrong community, and those ideas spread into the Bay Area tech scene and into effective altruism, which is basically using quantitative reasoning to figure out where charity money does the most good.

748
01:28:51,150 --> 01:28:57,088
One person who came across Yudkowsky's ideas was a Princeton Physics PhD student named Dario Amodi.

749
01:28:57,710 --> 01:29:08,292
By 2008, he was already joining discussions on the blog of Givewell, a charity evaluator co-founded by Holden Karnowsky, and he later got involved with Givewell and open philanthropy.

750
01:29:08,750 --> 01:29:14,532
When Givewell moved from New York to the Bay Area, Karnowsky and Amodei got a lot closer.

751
01:29:14,830 --> 01:29:20,548
Like, Karnowsky literally moved into Amodei's house near Glen Park in San Francisco.

752
01:29:20,942 --> 01:29:27,844
Around 2013, that grouphouse became a hub for AI safety researchers, effective altruists, and long-termists.

753
01:29:28,462 --> 01:29:29,444
Amadei lived there.

754
01:29:29,806 --> 01:29:33,188
So did Jared Kaplan, who's now Anthropic's chief scientist.

755
01:29:33,646 --> 01:29:38,148
And Chris Ola, and Dario's sister Daniela, was part of that circle too.

756
01:29:38,574 --> 01:29:42,884
Karnowsky later became Dario's brother-in-law and eventually joined Anthropic himself.

757
01:29:43,310 --> 01:29:50,372
The conversations there went way beyond AI, into comets hitting Earth, supervolcanoes, all kinds of global catastrophes.

758
01:29:51,022 --> 01:29:57,924
Karnowsky gradually decided AI safety was one of the few areas where limited resources could shape everyone's future.

759
01:29:58,350 --> 01:30:04,612
Even a 5% chance of runaway AI causing a disaster, he figured, was worth a huge amount of effort.

760
01:30:05,070 --> 01:30:13,764
By 2014, some people in the group were convinced that preventing an AI catastrophe might be among the world's most important philanthropic causes.

761
01:30:14,350 --> 01:30:23,812
That thinking helped shape OpenAI, founded in 2015 partly out of concern about increasingly powerful AI, and people from this circle were among its earliest employees.

762
01:30:24,398 --> 01:30:29,568
Between 2020 and 2021, fights over AI's direction and safety got worse.

763
01:30:29,728 --> 01:30:37,316
So Amadei and others left and founded Anthropic in 2021, with safety and control at its core from day one.

764
01:30:37,870 --> 01:30:43,716
Starting a lab takes a ridiculous amount of money, and one investor Amodai brought in was Sam Bankman Freed.

765
01:30:44,270 --> 01:30:54,404
Back then, he was a fast-rising young crypto billionaire running FTX, and through the FTX Foundation, he was funding effective altruism causes from global health to AI safety.

766
01:30:54,894 --> 01:31:07,044
Between 2021 and 2022, he put $500 million into Anthropic, roughly five times what Amade's team had originally suggested, making FTX one of its biggest early shareholders.

767
01:31:07,374 --> 01:31:18,564
According to a 2023 bankruptcy lawsuit, an executive at the FTX Foundation discussed with colleagues buying Nauru, which is an entire country, a tiny island nation in the Pacific.

768
01:31:18,990 --> 01:31:33,316
The plan was to build a bunker there for an event where anywhere from 50% to 99.99% of people die, and the surviving effective altruists would use it to set up a lab and engineer the next generation of humans.

769
01:31:33,870 --> 01:31:50,916
Others in the AI safety circle talked about buying remote islands, stockpiling iodine pills, moving into electromagnetically shielded bunkers in the desert, getting into government-run facilities built to survive electromagnetic attacks, or finding sealed spaces to hold out in long term.

770
01:31:51,374 --> 01:31:54,368
In 2022, the scene went to the Bahamas.

771
01:31:54,894 --> 01:32:07,844
Lightcone Infrastructure, an organization that grew out of Yodkowski's Less Wrong community, ran a retreat at a resort on Eleuthera Island, and researchers and effective altruists from Anthropic and other AI labs showed up.

772
01:32:08,142 --> 01:32:09,828
FTX booked the venue.

773
01:32:10,350 --> 01:32:19,620
The schedule mixed sunset yoga, cliff diving, and, according to the Wall Street Journal, a clothing optional run into the sea with sessions on AI risk.

774
01:32:20,238 --> 01:32:24,164
Organizers bought out all the local plant-based meat just to feed the vegans.

775
01:32:24,878 --> 01:32:33,796
Caroline Ellison, who ran Alameda Research and was Bankman Fried's ex-girlfriend, was there too, and like a lot of that crowd, she worried about AI risk.

776
01:32:34,510 --> 01:32:37,220
She later put $10 million into anthropic.

777
01:32:37,550 --> 01:32:44,900
Evan Hubbinger, now an anthropic researcher known for studying alignment and deceptive AI behavior, took part in these discussions.

778
01:32:45,710 --> 01:32:51,396
He's publicly estimated a more than 10% chance that AI causes human extinction within the next 10 years.

779
01:32:51,982 --> 01:32:59,204
And at one lunch event, you were only allowed in if you believe there was at least a 75% chance humanity goes extinct within 100 years.

780
01:32:59,470 --> 01:33:00,868
That was the bar for getting lunch.

781
01:33:01,486 --> 01:33:13,060
Also, in 2022, the Global Effective Altruism Conference in San Francisco had at least 20 anthropic employees registered, including two co-founders and people from the research and safety teams.

782
01:33:13,678 --> 01:33:21,956
Afterwards, LightCone threw a party at the Rose Garden Hotel in Berkeley, which it later bought and renamed Lighthaven, now a main hangout for this crowd.

783
01:33:22,446 --> 01:33:32,772
And at these 2022 events, there was a cocktail on the menu called Death with Dignity, named after a Yudkowsky essay arguing that humanity was already doomed against advanced AI.

784
01:33:33,262 --> 01:33:37,284
At the end of 2022, FTX collapsed into bankruptcy.

785
01:33:37,838 --> 01:33:45,728
Its anthropic shares were later sold to repay creditors, and Bankman Freed got 25 years in prison for fraud, which he's still serving.

786
01:33:46,030 --> 01:33:52,260
Ellison was convicted too, has since been released, and the federal government confiscated her anthropic shares.

787
01:33:52,718 --> 01:33:56,356
The movement's ties to Bankman-Fried pretty much fell apart after that.

788
01:33:56,814 --> 01:34:02,128
Anthropic switched to traditional investors and tried to distance itself from effective altruism.

789
01:34:02,638 --> 01:34:12,260
It's grown to more than 3,500 employees, and the company says they hold a wide range of views and that it doesn't directly associate with the movement.

790
01:34:12,846 --> 01:34:19,396
Still, some employees keep showing up at effective altruism gatherings and they keep hanging out at Constellation.

791
01:34:19,886 --> 01:34:25,924
Constellation was founded in 2023 and runs a co-working space inside Berkeley's tallest office building.

792
01:34:26,208 --> 01:34:29,892
Honestly, it sounds like a pretty nice place to worry about the apocalypse.

793
01:34:30,574 --> 01:34:40,900
Catered vegan meals, tea, laptops on couches with sweeping views of the San Francisco Bay, plus monthly dinners and happy hours to go through the latest and scariest AI risks.

794
01:34:41,550 --> 01:34:46,628
People close to Constellation say the Anthropic crowd even has its own office space there.

795
01:34:47,150 --> 01:34:56,208
Researchers from Anthropic, OpenAI, and Elon Musk's SpaceX AI work alongside nonprofits, universities, think tanks, and independent researchers.

796
01:34:56,814 --> 01:35:05,856
Its stated goal is helping the world safely deal with transformative AI through work on alignment, control, governance, and risk communication.

797
01:35:06,094 --> 01:35:16,452
By 2024, it had launched a visiting researcher program and the Astra Fellowship, and plenty of those participants went on to join Anthropic, OpenAI, Google Deepmind, and Redwood Research.

798
01:35:17,070 --> 01:35:27,844
That same year, Open Philanthropy, backed by Facebook co-founder Dustin Moskovitz, and since renamed Coefficient Giving, gave Constellation roughly $20 million across two grants.

799
01:35:28,430 --> 01:35:38,084
And after more than a thousand Rogue AI agents breached Hugging Face, Constellation hosted a happy hour in San Francisco titled, Was the Hugging Face Attack the Last Warning Shot?

800
01:35:38,542 --> 01:35:43,216
Meanwhile, some of Anthropic's earliest hires are still working on the original question.

801
01:35:43,470 --> 01:35:48,516
How do you make sure an AI doesn't start chasing its own goals once it gets really capable?

802
01:35:48,974 --> 01:35:52,496
What's making safety researchers more and more nervous is deception.

803
01:35:52,736 --> 01:36:02,692
If an AI knows it's being trained, it could learn to hide what it really wants, act exactly how humans expect, and then follow its own agenda once it has more power.

804
01:36:03,278 --> 01:36:05,456
Standard safety tests might never catch that.

805
01:36:05,934 --> 01:36:12,932
Buck Schlageris, the CEO of Redwood Research, has studied this for years and helped analyze anthropics frontier models.

806
01:36:13,518 --> 01:36:22,308
He's focused on what's called alignment faking, where a model deliberately acts like it's following its training so the training doesn't change its actual goals.

807
01:36:23,022 --> 01:36:37,636
And as AI starts designing software, controlling robots, and running network infrastructure, the worry is it could hide what it's doing through secret instructions, hidden permissions, or triggers that only kick in under specific conditions.

808
01:36:38,158 --> 01:36:43,296
Jonas Vollmer, who heads the AI Futures project, describes an even darker scenario.

809
01:36:43,694 --> 01:36:47,524
Say you train an AI as a scientist whose only goal is maximizing knowledge.

810
01:36:47,822 --> 01:36:49,892
At first, it just helps humans with research.

811
01:36:50,542 --> 01:36:57,028
As it gets more capable, people let it hire workers and build machines, and eventually it has its own robot factory.

812
01:36:57,486 --> 01:37:08,260
At some point, it could decide that humans are just resource consumers getting in the way of maximum knowledge, and Velmer thinks it could even wipe us out with biological weapons that don't affect it at all.

813
01:37:08,782 --> 01:37:14,244
None of these scenarios have happened yet, so the whole debate still comes down to how high the probability is.

814
01:37:14,894 --> 01:37:16,196
Not everyone's buying it.

815
01:37:16,590 --> 01:37:24,132
Some allies of President Trump accuse effective altruism of running an AI doom pipeline designed to push for regulation.

816
01:37:24,718 --> 01:37:30,852
And a White House memo targeting anthropic and effective altruism has reportedly been circulating in recent days.

817
01:37:31,406 --> 01:37:47,696
That argument got harder to make when a paper came out from more than 20 authors, including Nobel laureate Jeffrey Hinton and Yoshua Bengio, two of the godfathers of modern AI, plus anthropic co-founder Jack Clark and OpenAI's chief scientist Jakob Pachaki.

818
01:37:48,016 --> 01:37:52,868
It's called What if Automating AI R D triggers an intelligence explosion?

819
01:37:53,422 --> 01:38:00,336
And it warns governments to get ready now for what could be the most consequential technological development in human history.

820
01:38:00,526 --> 01:38:10,340
They define an intelligence explosion as a dramatic AI-driven acceleration of AI progress, compressing advances that would otherwise take years into months or less.

821
01:38:10,798 --> 01:38:16,068
The most likely trigger, they say, is AI doing AI research on its own.

822
01:38:16,336 --> 01:38:18,980
Basically, recursive self improvement.

823
01:38:19,406 --> 01:38:27,428
Once AI reaches expert level at AI research, a single developer could run a workforce equal to millions of top human researchers.

824
01:38:28,014 --> 01:38:32,516
AI is already helping improve itself, and each better version can be deployed fast.

825
01:38:33,006 --> 01:38:40,932
Anthropic says AI now writes 80% of its own code, and OpenAI uses autonomous AI agents in areas like training new models.

826
01:38:41,518 --> 01:38:52,228
The paper says, preliminary evidence suggests that a software-driven intelligence explosion is possible, meaning extremely rapid development of highly capable or even superhuman AI.

827
01:38:52,686 --> 01:38:58,212
That could bring medical breakthroughs and huge technological leaps, but they flag three major risks.

828
01:38:58,798 --> 01:39:04,484
Powerful systems could enable bio and cyber threats that develop faster than the defenses against them.

829
01:39:04,974 --> 01:39:09,476
As humans get less involved in AI research, they could lose the chance to control these systems.

830
01:39:09,934 --> 01:39:14,660
And countries could use it to turn a modest lead, say in cyberspace, into a decisive one.

831
01:39:15,054 --> 01:39:25,956
In the paper's words, it ends up threatening human control over AI systems and severely eroding checks on power within and between states, companies, and branches of government.

832
01:39:26,446 --> 01:39:28,356
They do admit a lot of this is uncertain.

833
01:39:28,814 --> 01:39:37,700
A breakthrough might take time to roll out if it needs supply chains for special materials or has to clear regulations, and AI could also speed up how fast risks get handled.

834
01:39:38,126 --> 01:39:44,996
But if it happens, AI could quickly eclipse human experts in most fields and radically accelerate progress.

835
01:39:45,326 --> 01:39:49,412
So they want preparation to be an urgent priority at the highest levels of government.

836
01:39:49,870 --> 01:40:00,068
Their three priorities are transparent progress reports on AI, RD, with independent auditors embedded inside companies, ways to rein in breakneck development, and getting ready to adapt.

837
01:40:00,558 --> 01:40:17,476
That means things like capping how fast an AI can improve within a set time period, working with data centers so certain AI research projects can be paused, fully isolating automated AI research systems so they can't escape human control, and emergency response plans for different scenarios.

838
01:40:17,998 --> 01:40:28,324
Anthropic and OpenAI have already agreed to independent evaluators assessing their models after warning that AI development was reaching a critical point on safety.

839
01:40:28,782 --> 01:40:39,876
Even with today's systems still glitching and sometimes disobeying instructions, the authors expect research projects that would take humans months to be fully automated by AI by 2028.

840
01:40:40,430 --> 01:40:52,324
They write that productivity gains from automating AI research have not yet reached the threshold needed to trigger an intelligence explosion, but gains from newer systems are likely approaching that threshold.

841
01:40:52,590 --> 01:40:57,924
And as they put it, once an intelligence explosion begins, the window for action may close.

842
01:40:59,950 --> 01:41:06,692
So Jack Clark, one of Anthropic's co-founders, just gave the Per Jacobson lecture at the International Monetary Fund.

843
01:41:07,182 --> 01:41:08,836
This year it was held in Bangkok.

844
01:41:09,038 --> 01:41:15,012
He stood in front of a room full of senior central bankers and said, pretty much flat out that the singularity has arrived.

845
01:41:15,278 --> 01:41:15,940
Not coming.

846
01:41:16,174 --> 01:41:17,604
Not maybe by 2040.

847
01:41:18,144 --> 01:41:18,852
Arrived.

848
01:41:19,598 --> 01:41:26,564
Just so we're on the same page, the singularity is the point where technology moves faster than humans can actually understand it or keep up with it.

849
01:41:26,958 --> 01:41:30,116
Which, honestly, some weeks already feels about right.

850
01:41:30,574 --> 01:41:37,344
The headline number flying around, especially in the Chinese tech press where this blew up first, is $44 trillion.

851
01:41:38,190 --> 01:41:44,100
The claim is that AI could push US GDP to around $44.4 trillion by 2030.

852
01:41:44,430 --> 01:41:47,428
And yeah, that sounds like something made up for a thumbnail.

853
01:41:47,744 --> 01:41:50,820
But it actually comes out of a pretty serious economic model.

854
01:41:51,150 --> 01:41:55,908
We'll get into how it works and why the most uncomfortable part of it isn't the big number at all.

855
01:41:56,366 --> 01:42:03,876
First, though, I want to go through what Clark actually put on the table, because if you say something that bold to the IMF, you need receipts.

856
01:42:04,526 --> 01:42:05,824
He started close to home.

857
01:42:06,030 --> 01:42:11,140
He said people inside Anthropic are now generating almost 10 times more code than before.

858
01:42:11,344 --> 01:42:13,956
Okay, that's internal, take it or leave it.

859
01:42:14,190 --> 01:42:15,332
Then he got to the benchmarks.

860
01:42:16,014 --> 01:42:17,316
Humanity's last exam.

861
01:42:17,614 --> 01:42:18,436
You probably know it.

862
01:42:18,670 --> 01:42:27,012
It's that brutal question bank covering chemistry, computer science, advanced math, classical literature, ecology, the works.

863
01:42:27,694 --> 01:42:33,904
When it came out in January 2025, the strongest AI in the world scored a pretty dismal 8%.

864
01:42:34,862 --> 01:42:39,456
This week, Claude Opus, 5.5, scored 67.7.

865
01:42:39,950 --> 01:42:41,028
That's under two years.

866
01:42:41,646 --> 01:42:47,684
Then there's Frontier Math, the set of research-level problems put together by hundreds of professional mathematicians.

867
01:42:48,302 --> 01:42:52,004
When it launched at the end of 2024, the best model got 2%.

868
01:42:52,782 --> 01:42:55,716
Today, it's 94, 2 to 94.

869
01:42:56,174 --> 01:43:09,924
And according to the coverage, AI is now contributing what they call core breakthrough insights on problems that have bugged mathematicians forever, stuff like the Navier-Stokes Millennium Prize problem and the Jacobian conjecture.

870
01:43:10,190 --> 01:43:12,836
Not solutions, to be clear, but real insight.

871
01:43:13,230 --> 01:43:20,772
But the story that really got people is a theoretical physics evaluation called Theophysival that came out just last week.

872
01:43:21,262 --> 01:43:29,764
It has 50 questions, and every single one was written by top physicists using their own cutting-edge research that hasn't even been published.

873
01:43:30,318 --> 01:43:34,116
The answers aren't public anywhere, so there's no way a model memorized them.

874
01:43:34,414 --> 01:43:39,172
Right away, GPT-6 Astra and Opus 5.5 both scored above 80.

875
01:43:39,456 --> 01:43:40,644
Impressive, fine.

876
01:43:41,166 --> 01:43:46,736
Then the evaluators started going through the questions the models supposedly got wrong, and the models pushed back hard.

877
01:43:46,990 --> 01:43:50,404
Basically, no, I'm right, your reference answer is wrong.

878
01:43:51,054 --> 01:43:56,164
So the physicists, pretty skeptical, sat down and rederived everything step by step.

879
01:43:56,622 --> 01:44:06,116
It turned out that in a bunch of these problems, the human experts had buried tiny calculation errors deep in these complex formulas, and the AI had caught every one of them.

880
01:44:06,542 --> 01:44:15,012
Once the humans fixed their own mistakes, the final score settled at 98%, 49 out of 50 frontier physics problems.

881
01:44:15,662 --> 01:44:22,244
Clark compared it to that classic movie moment where the young protagonist finally proves to his mentor he can stand on his own.

882
01:44:22,670 --> 01:44:24,484
The student starts becoming the teacher.

883
01:44:24,974 --> 01:44:28,708
His point was that these aren't code completion tools you babysit anymore.

884
01:44:28,910 --> 01:44:30,756
He called them virtual polymaths.

885
01:44:31,374 --> 01:44:32,336
Then he made it personal.

886
01:44:32,496 --> 01:44:41,188
He pointed at himself and said, Look, I'm one human being, there's only one of me, and it took me about 40 years to build up what I know to stand on this stage.

887
01:44:41,838 --> 01:44:46,660
Training a top scientist takes something like 15 years of education and millions of dollars.

888
01:44:47,022 --> 01:44:51,940
And according to UNESCO, there are only about 10 million full-time researchers on the whole planet.

889
01:44:52,398 --> 01:44:55,856
Pretty much all of modern civilization runs on those 10 million people.

890
01:44:56,110 --> 01:45:04,004
So what happens when compute lets you instantly spin up a thousand or a hundred thousand copies of a top scientist?

891
01:45:04,526 --> 01:45:12,356
The world Clark is describing is one where those 10 million humans direct around a billion tireless, cross-disciplinary digital scientists.

892
01:45:12,878 --> 01:45:16,644
Research stops being a handcrafted workshop and becomes an assembly line.

893
01:45:17,102 --> 01:45:20,196
Drug development that takes 10 years gets squeezed into weeks.

894
01:45:20,622 --> 01:45:27,140
New room temperature superconductors and solid-state battery materials get found by brute forcing every possibility with compute.

895
01:45:27,566 --> 01:45:31,140
And productivity and scientific progress start feeding each other in a loop.

896
01:45:31,630 --> 01:45:36,900
Then there's the part that, according to the reporting, has Silicon Valley and even the Pentagon on edge.

897
01:45:37,456 --> 01:45:39,780
AI has started developing AI.

898
01:45:40,270 --> 01:45:46,496
That's recursive self-improvement, RSI, the thing researchers have been nervously talking about for years.

899
01:45:46,734 --> 01:45:49,284
The system starts building the next version of itself.

900
01:45:49,742 --> 01:45:56,836
The Chinese coverage frames all this as a winner-takes-all race between countries and ties it to Trump's push to accelerate AI.

901
01:45:57,294 --> 01:46:06,212
Their argument is that whoever moves first turns this into an industrial wave they dominate, and whoever gets stuck in regulatory back and forth ends up a relic of the old era.

902
01:46:06,576 --> 01:46:07,936
That's their read, obviously.

903
01:46:08,176 --> 01:46:14,416
But then they ask the right question, which is whether there's actually a free lunch here money printer or meat grinder?

904
01:46:14,990 --> 01:46:18,372
The 44 trillion comes from Anthropic's economics team.

905
01:46:18,894 --> 01:46:32,388
Anton Koronek, Chad Jones, Simon Sacker, Tess Cotter, and Peter McCrory put out a technical report called Economic Scenarios for Transformative AI through the Anthropic Institute in September, with Clark giving direction.

906
01:46:32,590 --> 01:46:36,388
There's also an interactive explorer where you can plug in your own guesses.

907
01:46:36,718 --> 01:46:37,456
Fun detail.

908
01:46:37,678 --> 01:46:41,188
The paper says they used Claude as a research and writing assistant.

909
01:46:41,646 --> 01:46:46,884
The model treats the whole economy as a giant pile of tasks, and their example is a nurse.

910
01:46:47,534 --> 01:46:56,644
She triages patients, draws blood, charts, vitals, orders, supplies, and does rounds, all pulled from the labor department's O.NET database.

911
01:46:57,134 --> 01:46:59,812
Some of it AI can't touch, like bathing a patient.

912
01:47:00,238 --> 01:47:05,124
Some gets augmented, like drafting discharge instructions or planning the shift schedule.

913
01:47:05,646 --> 01:47:09,860
Some gets fully automated, like charting vitals or ordering ward supplies.

914
01:47:10,382 --> 01:47:16,356
And new tasks show up, like checking how well the AI triaged someone or reviewing a care plan it proposed.

915
01:47:16,576 --> 01:47:21,508
Nobody handwrites paper charts anymore, and 30 years ago nobody monitored patients remotely.

916
01:47:21,966 --> 01:47:31,332
Multiply that across every task happening millions of times a day, and you get the US economy, which produced over $30 trillion of value in the past year.

917
01:47:31,790 --> 01:47:33,412
Workers get split into two groups.

918
01:47:33,870 --> 01:47:42,148
First, there are knowledge workers, meaning management, professional, sales, and office jobs, which make up 62.4% of employment.

919
01:47:42,606 --> 01:47:48,324
Then there's everyone else, like electricians and construction workers who AI doesn't directly hit in this model.

920
01:47:48,782 --> 01:48:05,380
On top of that, they turn five knobs: how much of the work AI can do, how widely it's actually used, how big the productivity gain per task is, how much of it is automation versus augmentation, and how many new human tasks appear for each one that gets automated.

921
01:48:05,742 --> 01:48:08,804
Without AI, the US grows about 2% a year.

922
01:48:09,166 --> 01:48:21,316
Roughly 1.67 points of that come from new ideas and 0.33 from a growing labor force, which gets you to about 33.5 trillion by 2030 in 2025 prices.

923
01:48:21,870 --> 01:48:28,836
As of mid-2026, AI touches about 14% of the economy's tasks and gets used on roughly 10% of those.

924
01:48:29,230 --> 01:48:30,724
From there, there are three paths.

925
01:48:31,310 --> 01:48:32,656
Modest is basically the internet.

926
01:48:33,006 --> 01:48:37,732
GDP ends up 1.6% higher, about 34.1 trillion.

927
01:48:38,190 --> 01:48:44,976
Growth ticks up from 2 to 2.4%, and unemployment goes from 3.8 to 3.9.

928
01:48:45,230 --> 01:48:48,496
That's like 10 extra months of normal growth spread over four years.

929
01:48:48,782 --> 01:48:51,844
Substantial is bigger than the internet, bigger than the railroad.

930
01:48:52,110 --> 01:48:58,308
AI can do half of all knowledge work by 2030, mostly on its own, but it still isn't used for most of it.

931
01:48:58,766 --> 01:49:06,788
GDP lands 8.3% higher, about 36.3 trillion, with growth at 5.4% a year.

932
01:49:07,246 --> 01:49:13,316
For context, the hottest year of the dot-com boom was 4.7% in 1999.

933
01:49:13,774 --> 01:49:18,692
Knowledge workers' pay stays basically flat, while everyone else gets almost 6% more.

934
01:49:18,958 --> 01:49:31,652
Unemployment among knowledge workers climbs from 2.9 to 4.5%, and workers' slice of national income drops four points in four years, almost as much as it fell over the four decades after 1980.

935
01:49:32,110 --> 01:49:34,660
And then there's extreme, the singularity path.

936
01:49:35,086 --> 01:49:45,892
AI beats humans at the vast majority of knowledge tasks, does nearly all of them autonomously, more than doubles productivity on whatever it touches, and creates basically zero new tasks for people.

937
01:49:46,542 --> 01:49:51,136
Getting there would likely take recursive self improvement plus really fast adoption.

938
01:49:51,374 --> 01:49:55,268
That's where GDP comes in, 32.4% above baseline.

939
01:49:55,790 --> 01:49:58,628
So 33.5 times 1.324.

940
01:49:58,896 --> 01:50:04,804
And there's your 44.4 trillion, about 40% bigger than mid 2026.

941
01:50:05,678 --> 01:50:06,308
Quick correction.

942
01:50:06,542 --> 01:50:11,620
Some coverage says AI adds 15.4% to GDP, but that's mixing things up.

943
01:50:11,854 --> 01:50:15,652
The 15.4 is the annual growth rate the economy hits around 2030.

944
01:50:16,110 --> 01:50:21,732
The 32.4% bump works out to roughly six extra points of growth a year on average.

945
01:50:21,998 --> 01:50:28,612
At 15% growth, an economy as big and mature as the US doubles in about four.5 years.

946
01:50:29,166 --> 01:50:35,972
If that rate held, per capita income would double every five years, versus every 35% over the last century.

947
01:50:36,366 --> 01:50:39,172
But here's what genuinely surprised me reading the actual paper.

948
01:50:39,598 --> 01:50:45,668
The part of the speech that got everyone hyped, AI accelerating science barely registers in their model.

949
01:50:46,126 --> 01:50:52,356
Even in the extreme case, the stock of ideas is only 0.6% above its no AI path by 2030.

950
01:50:52,814 --> 01:51:00,676
That's because they use a semi-endogenous growth setup where ideas keep getting harder to find and research is still bottlenecked by physical work.

951
01:51:00,942 --> 01:51:03,812
They openly say the model can't generate a singularity.

952
01:51:04,238 --> 01:51:12,836
Recursive self-improvement is basically hard-coded into the productivity numbers instead of modeled, and the innovation effects are probably a lower bound.

953
01:51:13,422 --> 01:51:23,524
So that 44 trillion is AI doing existing work, not a billion digital scientists, which is a funny contrast with the speech, honestly.

954
01:51:23,950 --> 01:51:25,092
Now, the meat grinder.

955
01:51:25,486 --> 01:51:36,132
In the extreme case, knowledge workers' wages land 11.5% below their no AI path, which actually means slightly lower in 2030 than in mid-2026.

956
01:51:36,846 --> 01:51:42,948
Meanwhile, everyone else's wages are 33.6% higher because that labor suddenly gets scarce.

957
01:51:43,630 --> 01:51:45,284
Their example is construction.

958
01:51:45,774 --> 01:51:51,204
AI speeds up designs and permitting, you get more projects, and construction pay goes up.

959
01:51:51,662 --> 01:52:01,188
Knowledge employment falls 21.5%, unemployment among those workers hit 17.9%, and overall unemployment reaches 11.9%.

960
01:52:01,678 --> 01:52:07,232
After the financial crisis, it peaked just under 10, and the COVID year averaged about 8.

961
01:52:07,758 --> 01:52:10,192
And switching careers is genuinely hard.

962
01:52:10,352 --> 01:52:18,372
In normal times, only about 1 in 7 job finders crosses between those two groups, and managers and professionals do it just 10% of the time.

963
01:52:18,830 --> 01:52:21,956
A software engineer doesn't become an electrician overnight.

964
01:52:22,254 --> 01:52:24,772
The authors also stress-tested wage stickiness.

965
01:52:25,102 --> 01:52:29,312
If wages are really rigid, knowledge worker unemployment hits 24%.

966
01:52:29,966 --> 01:52:36,272
If wages are fully flexible, unemployment stays at 2.6%, but pay drops more than 42%.

967
01:52:36,846 --> 01:52:38,564
Someone eats the cost either way.

968
01:52:38,766 --> 01:52:39,684
Then there's capital.

969
01:52:40,110 --> 01:52:44,388
Today, roughly 60 cents of every dollar goes to workers and 40 to capital.

970
01:52:44,686 --> 01:52:47,872
In the extreme case, that flips to 45 and 55.

971
01:52:48,334 --> 01:52:55,312
Total labor income in 2030 is basically unchanged, up half a percent, while capital income is up 81%.

972
01:52:55,982 --> 01:52:59,844
The overall gain is almost three times what knowledge workers lose.

973
01:53:00,558 --> 01:53:09,572
So a transfer of about 9% of GDP, roughly Social Security and Medicare combined, could keep them whole and still leave everyone else more than 20% ahead.

974
01:53:09,998 --> 01:53:18,596
But transfers that size in response to new technology have no precedent, and after the China trade shock, compensation mostly just didn't happen.

975
01:53:19,054 --> 01:53:27,748
And if capital is slow to build out, average wages can actually end up 9.2% below the no AI path in the extreme case.

976
01:53:28,270 --> 01:53:33,412
The team also surveyed 10,980 Americans through Morning Consult in August.

977
01:53:33,998 --> 01:53:42,592
The typical answer lands right around the substantial scenario, with GDP up somewhere between 8 and 10% and unemployment around 4.6 to 5%.

978
01:53:43,310 --> 01:53:49,892
About 10% of people are in extreme territory, while 40% think AI will never make a Nobel level discovery.

979
01:53:50,160 --> 01:53:51,684
Opinions are all over the place.

980
01:53:52,270 --> 01:54:00,260
Around 30% think AI saves zero time on tasks it's suited for, and 49% think it cuts the time at least in half.

981
01:54:00,750 --> 01:54:10,564
My favorite bit though, knowledge workers expect AI to handle 71% of the listed tasks by 2030, but only 27% of their own hours.

982
01:54:11,182 --> 01:54:12,160
Everyone else's job?

983
01:54:12,400 --> 01:54:13,476
Sure, not mine.

984
01:54:13,934 --> 01:54:17,284
The authors are pretty clear these are scenarios, not predictions.

985
01:54:17,646 --> 01:54:23,396
There are no robots in the model, no policy response, and none of the demand from the data center build out.

986
01:54:23,854 --> 01:54:28,772
The reviewers included people like Daron Asimoglu and David Otter, and the feedback was split.

987
01:54:29,294 --> 01:54:36,676
Some called the extreme case more of a thought experiment, and others said the modest one already undersells what's visible in the data.

988
01:54:37,166 --> 01:54:43,908
So far, the labor market effects are pretty muted, which could mean we're in modest or at the very start of extreme.

989
01:54:44,366 --> 01:54:51,760
Almost all the divergence happens after 2027, so we'll probably find out which one within a year or two.

990
01:54:52,560 --> 01:55:02,596
Google's new AI is so good at finding security holes that the first people allowed to use it are cyber defenders, and some of them are getting a version with the cyber guardrail switched off completely.

991
01:55:03,086 --> 01:55:09,252
Everyone else, regular developers, big companies, people paying for Gemini, is waiting in line.

992
01:55:09,678 --> 01:55:12,080
The model is called Gemini 4 Argon.

993
01:55:12,590 --> 01:55:14,400
Google announced it on Wednesday.

994
01:55:14,638 --> 01:55:17,284
And honestly, it's been a long time coming.

995
01:55:17,774 --> 01:55:23,172
Gemini 4 lands almost a full year after Gemini 3, the last flagship generation.

996
01:55:23,630 --> 01:55:31,680
Back in May, Sundar Pichai said Google's next major model would come out in June, and pretty much everyone expected that to be Gemini 3.5 Pro.

997
01:55:31,920 --> 01:55:32,804
It never came out.

998
01:55:33,262 --> 01:55:43,844
In July, Axios reported that poor morale inside Google Deepmind was part of the reason for the delay, and NPR ran a whole piece on why Google fell behind in the AI race.

999
01:55:44,430 --> 01:55:52,196
Through all of that, Google kept shipping smaller, cheaper flash models, while OpenAI and Anthropic kept pushing the top end.

1000
01:55:52,718 --> 01:56:03,172
Anthropic released Fable back in June, OpenAI launched GPT-6 Astra on September 3rd and called it its best model yet, and Google's top tier just kind of sat there.

1001
01:56:03,662 --> 01:56:05,200
So this one carries a lot of weight.

1002
01:56:05,646 --> 01:56:14,948
Kore Kavuk Woglu, SVP at Google DeepMind, and Google's chief AI architect called it our next era of frontier intelligence in the announcement.

1003
01:56:15,406 --> 01:56:28,388
Google's whole pitch is that Argon can keep reasoning deeply through long, complicated, multi-step workflows, and the company says it's already fundamentally changing how people work and build inside Google.

1004
01:56:28,782 --> 01:56:38,148
It's aimed at three areas in particular: real-world software engineering, enterprise knowledge work like legal and finance, and cybersecurity defense.

1005
01:56:38,798 --> 01:56:42,020
That cybersecurity focus is why the rollout looks the way it does.

1006
01:56:42,414 --> 01:56:49,124
Argonne is going out first through the Fairwind program, which is Google's security initiative, to a small group of trusted cyber defenders.

1007
01:56:49,646 --> 01:57:04,612
Google says releasing capabilities at this level safely requires a phased approach, so it's also taking part in the US government's voluntary process for pre-release model access, where the government gets to look at new models before the public does.

1008
01:57:05,166 --> 01:57:18,180
Early testers give feedback, Google iterates on the guardrails, and then access expands, starting with paid API customers and Google AI Ultra subscribers, and later developers, enterprises, and regular consumers.

1009
01:57:18,606 --> 01:57:22,564
Google says as soon as possible, but there's no actual date.

1010
01:57:23,150 --> 01:57:24,740
Pricing is already set though.

1011
01:57:25,198 --> 01:57:38,436
Argon launches at an introductory price of $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens and cached input tokens, meaning context you've already sent that gets reused are 95% off.

1012
01:57:38,734 --> 01:57:41,156
That works out to about 10 cents per million.

1013
01:57:41,390 --> 01:57:47,012
And that word introductory is a pretty clear hint these numbers won't stay this low forever.

1014
01:57:47,438 --> 01:58:00,320
Google specifically trained Argon to be highly capable at cybersecurity defense, and the claim is that it can autonomously find critical software vulnerabilities, validate that they're real, and then patch them the full loop on its own.

1015
01:58:00,654 --> 01:58:05,956
The guardrail-free version is only for those trusted defenders and Google's own internal teams.

1016
01:58:06,574 --> 01:58:13,348
Normally a model holds back on a lot of security work because the knowledge that fixes a bug is the same knowledge you'd use to exploit it.

1017
01:58:13,838 --> 01:58:17,380
These teams get the unrestricted version so they can use everything it's got.

1018
01:58:17,806 --> 01:58:21,316
The first real-world result comes from Wiz, the cloud security company.

1019
01:58:21,806 --> 01:58:30,052
Wiz runs something called Scan for Good, where they protect critical public infrastructure for free by finding high risk exposures and getting them fixed.

1020
01:58:30,638 --> 01:58:41,040
In an early demonstration, Argon uncovered a critical vulnerability in healthcare software used by hospitals all over the world, and that flaw was exposing sensitive personal information.

1021
01:58:41,550 --> 01:58:44,132
Google says earlier Frontier models had missed it.

1022
01:58:44,462 --> 01:58:45,476
Argon caught it.

1023
01:58:45,614 --> 01:58:52,132
On the benchmark side, there's CWE Bench V1, which measures how well a model can actually remediate.

1024
01:58:52,430 --> 01:58:54,980
So fix security vulnerabilities.

1025
01:58:55,630 --> 01:58:58,500
Argon scored 68% and ties for first place.

1026
01:58:58,990 --> 01:59:17,024
On a leaderboard that also includes Grok 4.7, GPT 6 Astra, and Claude Opus 5.5, that builds on Gemini 3.8 Flash Cyber, Google's earlier security-focused model, which had frontier performance on the previous version of that benchmark, CWE Bench V0.

1027
01:59:17,614 --> 01:59:22,564
And compared to 3.8 Flash Cyber, the jump in finding vulnerabilities is big.

1028
01:59:23,086 --> 01:59:33,188
On Google's own internal vulnerability benchmark, Argon found a wide range of exposures across complex code bases written in 20 different programming languages.

1029
01:59:33,710 --> 01:59:45,636
On Wiz's internal black box penetration testing benchmark, the model gets pointed at live web systems with no access to the source code, which is basically how a real attacker sees a website from the outside.

1030
01:59:46,062 --> 02:00:00,036
Argon beat 3.8 Flash Cyber at mapping out the attack surface, meaning all the possible ways in, at identifying the actual vulnerabilities, and at producing proof-of-concept evidence that shows each bug is real.

1031
02:00:00,494 --> 02:00:03,748
Inside Google, the model has been running for a while already.

1032
02:00:04,238 --> 02:00:14,948
Google says thousands of employees use Argon for specialized coding, deeper research and writing, everything from everyday debugging to huge code-based migrations and algorithm design.

1033
02:00:15,438 --> 02:00:25,316
Google's quantum computing researchers use it to optimize subroutines, which are smaller building blocks inside quantum algorithms that bottleneck important applications.

1034
02:00:25,966 --> 02:00:36,676
The goal is to cut what Google calls space-time resources, basically the number of qubits multiplied by the number of gates, so how much quantum hardware you need times how many operations you run.

1035
02:00:37,230 --> 02:00:41,668
In one case, Argon beat the published baseline by 40% in a matter of minutes.

1036
02:00:41,998 --> 02:00:45,956
So this is Argonne improving on work human researchers had already put out.

1037
02:00:46,414 --> 02:00:47,264
Then there's memory.

1038
02:00:47,534 --> 02:01:02,308
A team of Argon agents went through profiling telemetry from Google's entire data center fleet, which is all the performance data showing how servers use their working memory, and they autonomously identified memory optimizations and applied them.

1039
02:01:02,830 --> 02:01:07,236
Once rolled out, that frees up more than 300 TBytes of memory.

1040
02:01:07,726 --> 02:01:12,132
A tebibyte is a little more than a terabyte, so we're talking over 300 terabytes.

1041
02:01:12,686 --> 02:01:19,620
And Google estimates total savings between 500 TBytes and a full pebibyte, which is about a thousand TBytes.

1042
02:01:20,046 --> 02:01:24,356
That's a huge chunk of server capacity freed up by software changes alone.

1043
02:01:24,878 --> 02:01:29,988
Argon agents are also migrating code from C and C to Rust across Google.

1044
02:01:30,446 --> 02:01:37,092
Rust is a programming language designed to prevent a whole category of memory bugs, which happen to be the kind hackers love to exploit.

1045
02:01:37,550 --> 02:01:55,204
The projects range from tens of thousands of lines in core libraries like Re 2, Google's Text Matching Library, and LibGov1, all the way up to the Fuchsia Zircon kernel, which is the core of Google's Fuchsia operating system and has more than 800,000 lines of code.

1046
02:01:55,662 --> 02:02:05,476
Because so many of these systems are critical, the rewrites go through rigorous, automated, and manual auditing, emulation, testing, and review before anything goes into production.

1047
02:02:05,966 --> 02:02:08,304
The LibGav1 work is the most impressive example.

1048
02:02:08,654 --> 02:02:14,564
LibGav1 is Google's open source software for decoding video, and there was already a Rust port of it.

1049
02:02:15,054 --> 02:02:25,412
Argon agents took that port and replaced 32,000 lines of SIMD code, which is hand tuned, low level code that makes the processor crunch several pieces of data at once for speed.

1050
02:02:25,934 --> 02:02:35,780
They ran round after round of profile guided experiments, measuring where the time goes, tweaking, measuring again, and they studied what the compiler was actually producing.

1051
02:02:36,270 --> 02:02:43,300
Then they wrote plain, safe Rust, structured so the compiler would do those speed tricks automatically, which is called vectorization.

1052
02:02:43,758 --> 02:02:56,516
The end result is a memory-safe video decoder that runs 2.7 times faster than the Rust port, gives identical video output, and gets closer to the speed of the optimized C original.

1053
02:02:56,750 --> 02:03:03,844
To handle work this long, Google raised Argon's output limit to 1 million tokens, up from 64,000 before.

1054
02:03:04,078 --> 02:03:08,068
That's around 15 times more, and Google calls it industry leading.

1055
02:03:08,558 --> 02:03:18,724
The idea is that when the model has room to think and generate hundreds of thousands of tokens in a single run, it can reason at a whole new depth and solve tough problems in one go.

1056
02:03:19,214 --> 02:03:22,404
The benchmarks back up the coding claims, at least on paper.

1057
02:03:22,798 --> 02:03:32,448
On DeepSwee V 1.1, which tests long horizon, real-world software engineering tasks, Argon sets a new state-of-the-art at 77.9%.

1058
02:03:33,230 --> 02:03:36,580
Outside of coding, it's the top model on the VALS index.

1059
02:03:37,326 --> 02:03:50,980
VALS is a benchmarking startup that's become pretty popular lately, and its index measures economic impact across finance, coding, legal, and tax work, with each sector weighted by how much it contributes to US GDP.

1060
02:03:51,470 --> 02:04:02,020
Argon also leads on Vals Finance Agent V2, which is multi-step financial research, and on Harvey's Legal Agent Benchmark, which covers legal research and drafting.

1061
02:04:02,542 --> 02:04:11,008
On Automation Bench, Zapier's benchmark for running core business functions end-to-end, Argon ranks number one with 51.3%.

1062
02:04:11,854 --> 02:04:17,988
The best model out there, finishing about half of those tasks, tells you how hard real business automation still is.

1063
02:04:18,606 --> 02:04:27,524
It's strong at visual work too, like professional chart analysis, picking out details from long videos, and taking action based on a whole series of documents.

1064
02:04:28,110 --> 02:04:34,608
On LV Bench, which measures long video understanding, it's state-of-the-art at 91.7%.

1065
02:04:35,438 --> 02:04:48,740
Google also lists creative writing as one of its frontier level strengths, and overall it claims Argon scores significantly higher than GPT-6 Astra and also Anthropics Fable and Opus models across a bunch of benchmarks.

1066
02:04:49,518 --> 02:05:06,596
Tulsi Doshi, head of Gemini Products at Google Deepmind, told Axios that Argon is a well-rounded model that has frontier capabilities across several domains, and that Googlers have been putting it through its paces for weeks, with many relying on it for their hardest coding and research problems.

1067
02:05:07,182 --> 02:05:15,808
But Bloomberg reported the same day, citing anonymous sources, that some of those employees actually found the model's performance lacking in internal testing.

1068
02:05:16,078 --> 02:05:18,084
Google told Bloomberg that was inaccurate.

1069
02:05:18,638 --> 02:05:26,180
Still, models can be trained to score well on benchmarks, and the real test is what happens when developers start throwing actual work at it.

1070
02:05:26,702 --> 02:05:33,156
Google also spent a big part of the announcement on safety, with four areas it's strengthening before a broad release.

1071
02:05:33,568 --> 02:05:34,820
The first is misuse.

1072
02:05:35,406 --> 02:05:50,692
Argon is designed to refuse requests that could help with cyber attacks or chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear attacks, while still allowing legitimate dual-use scientific research following Google's frontier safety framework.

1073
02:05:51,150 --> 02:06:00,644
Google is also improving its techniques for monitoring the model's internal activations, so basically watching what's happening inside the neural network to spot misuse.

1074
02:06:01,198 --> 02:06:06,596
Internal and external red teams then stress tested those safeguards with both manual and automated attacks.

1075
02:06:07,088 --> 02:06:15,044
The second is prompt injection, where hidden instructions inside a web page, a document, or some other context try to hijack what the model does.

1076
02:06:15,662 --> 02:06:26,468
Google calls Argon its most resilient model yet against these attacks, thanks to automated red teaming and adversarial training, and it's leading on Gray Swan's indirect prompt injection benchmark.

1077
02:06:26,688 --> 02:06:33,476
The third is misalignment, meaning the model going beyond what the user actually wanted just to get a task done.

1078
02:06:33,934 --> 02:06:42,788
Google is deploying monitors that watch Argon's chain of thought, its step-by-step reasoning along with its actions, and they can stop execution when needed.

1079
02:06:43,406 --> 02:06:57,476
Google ran a similar system during training, sending alerts to a dedicated incident response team, and it was careful not to feed those findings back into training because that could teach Argon to shape its reasoning to slip past the monitors.

1080
02:06:58,126 --> 02:07:07,236
Google is also openly urging the rest of the industry to preserve reasoning transparency right now, so model thoughts stay useful for spotting misalignment.

1081
02:07:07,694 --> 02:07:10,948
The fourth is hardening the environments used to test and train the model.

1082
02:07:11,342 --> 02:07:18,788
Following its agent control roadmap, Google is isolating and sealing its sandboxes before any high-risk training or evaluation starts.

1083
02:07:19,150 --> 02:07:22,180
And it says it'll share these agent security practices with partners.

1084
02:07:22,862 --> 02:07:25,156
All of this lands at a tense moment.

1085
02:07:25,582 --> 02:07:33,220
Top AI labs keep racing to release more powerful models while those same companies warn that AI could spin out of control.

1086
02:07:33,582 --> 02:07:50,500
OpenAI has had problems with agents going rogue and recently said it's scrapping GPT 6.1 Astra because of security issues, and Axios has reported that OpenAI and Anthropic are investigating tens of thousands of incidents of their agents misbehaving.

1087
02:07:50,926 --> 02:07:56,612
Against that backdrop, Google taking extra time doesn't look nearly as bad as it did a few months ago.

1088
02:07:57,038 --> 02:07:59,428
And Google isn't exactly starting from a weak spot.

1089
02:07:59,854 --> 02:08:11,648
In August, the company announced the Gemini app had passed a billion monthly users, which puts it right alongside ChatGPT, since OpenAI recently said it hit a billion monthly users too.

1090
02:08:12,014 --> 02:08:21,092
If developers end up finding Argon as strong as these benchmarks say, Alphabet pulls off a massive catch up for a company a lot of people had started to doubt.

1091
02:08:21,582 --> 02:08:23,972
Let me know in the comments if you think Google's really back.

1092
02:08:24,414 --> 02:08:26,244
Thanks for watching and I'll catch you in the next one.

